INSERT INTO public.community_translations (community_id, locale, name, short_description, description)
VALUES
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'al-barsha'), 'ae', 'Al Barsha', 'A well-established, centrally located residential community offering spacious homes, excellent amenities, and everyday convenience in the heart of Dubai.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Al Barsha is one of Dubai’s most practical and liveable residential areas, known for its balance between affordability, space, and connectivity. Located just south of Sheikh Zayed Road, it has evolved from a quiet suburban district into a vibrant, mixed-use community popular with families, professionals, and long-term residents. Its proximity to major business hubs, schools, hospitals, and retail centres makes it one of the most functional places to live in Dubai.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>Al Barsha is home to Mall of the Emirates, one of Dubai’s largest shopping and entertainment destinations, featuring international brands, restaurants, cafés, cinemas, and Ski Dubai. The surrounding streets are filled with neighbourhood cafés, casual dining spots, supermarkets, and everyday services. Nightlife is more relaxed and community-focused, with hotel lounges and local restaurants rather than large clubs.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>The area offers access to Al Barsha Pond Park, a popular green space with walking tracks, play areas, and fitness zones. Many residential streets are quieter than central districts, making them suitable for walking, jogging, and family activities. Gyms, yoga studios, and sports facilities are widely available.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>Al Barsha benefits from excellent road connectivity via Sheikh Zayed Road, Al Khail Road, and Umm Suqeim Road. The Dubai Metro runs along Sheikh Zayed Road with multiple stations serving the area, providing easy access to Dubai Marina, Downtown Dubai, and DIFC. Travel times across the city are generally efficient.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>Al Barsha is one of Dubai’s most family-friendly areas due to the concentration of reputable schools, nurseries, and learning centres offering British, IB, American, and Indian curricula. The community is also well-served by hospitals, clinics, pharmacies, and specialist medical centres. Daily essentials are easily accessible, with supermarkets, salons, fitness centres, and service providers spread throughout the neighbourhood.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>The area features a mix of low-rise apartment buildings, mid-rise residential blocks, and spacious villas, particularly in Al Barsha 1, 2, and 3. Homes here typically offer larger layouts compared to newer high-density districts, with many villas including private gardens and parking.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>Al Barsha attracts steady rental demand from families and professionals seeking space and convenience at more reasonable price points. While capital appreciation may be slower than in premium waterfront districts, rental yields are stable due to long-term tenants and consistent demand. It is considered a low-volatility, income-focused investment area.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>Limited luxury branding compared to waterfront or master-planned communities</li><li>Fewer new off-plan developments, with much of the area already built-up</li><li>Traffic congestion can occur during peak hours near major malls and schools</li><li>Less nightlife and entertainment compared to Marina or Downtown</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Al Barsha?</h4><p>Al Barsha is ideal for residents and investors prioritising space, convenience, and everyday livability over spectacle. It offers a grounded, community-driven lifestyle with strong infrastructure, excellent connectivity, and reliable rental demand. For those seeking a central Dubai location that feels practical, established, and family-oriented, Al Barsha remains one of the city’s most dependable choices.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'al-fahidi'), 'ae', 'Al Fahidi', 'A culturally rich historic neighbourhood offering traditional architecture, heritage homes, and an authentic glimpse into old Dubai by the Creek.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Al Fahidi, also known as the Al Fahidi Historical Neighbourhood or Al Bastakiya, is one of Dubai’s oldest and most culturally significant districts. Dating back to the late 19th century, the area preserves the city’s traditional Emirati architecture, narrow sikkas, and iconic wind-tower houses. Located along Dubai Creek in Bur Dubai, Al Fahidi offers a unique contrast to the city’s modern skyline and attracts residents, creatives, and visitors seeking heritage, art, and history.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>Shopping in Al Fahidi focuses on boutique stores, art spaces, cultural centres, and traditional markets rather than large malls. Nearby Meena Bazaar and Bur Dubai souks provide access to textiles, spices, gold, and local goods. Dining options include heritage cafés, Emirati restaurants, and independent eateries. Nightlife is minimal, with the area favouring quiet evenings, cultural events, and courtyard gatherings.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>The neighbourhood is highly walkable, with shaded lanes and restored courtyards designed for pedestrian exploration. Proximity to Dubai Creek allows for scenic walks, abra rides, and waterfront experiences. Art exhibitions, cultural festivals, and open-air events regularly take place within the district.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>Al Fahidi is well connected via Al Fahidi Metro Station on the Green Line, as well as major roads linking to Deira, Downtown Dubai, and Sheikh Zayed Road. Water transport via abras across Dubai Creek adds both practicality and charm, connecting residents to Deira in minutes.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>While Al Fahidi itself is more cultural than residential-heavy, surrounding Bur Dubai areas provide access to schools, clinics, pharmacies, supermarkets, and essential services. The district is best suited to residents who value proximity to amenities rather than having them directly within the community. Healthcare facilities and educational institutions are easily reachable within a short drive.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>Residential options are limited and highly specialised, consisting mainly of restored heritage homes, courtyard houses, and low-rise traditional buildings. Many properties are repurposed for cultural, hospitality, or creative uses rather than conventional long-term residential living.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>Al Fahidi appeals more to niche investors than mass-market buyers. Demand is driven by boutique hospitality, museums, art galleries, cafés, and cultural institutions. Short-term and experiential stays perform better than traditional rentals, particularly those targeting tourists and culture-focused visitors.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>Very limited residential inventory</li><li>Not suited for modern high-rise or luxury living preferences</li><li>Minimal nightlife and entertainment infrastructure</li><li>Heritage preservation rules restrict renovations and redevelopment</li><li>Less convenient for families seeking large homes or school-centric living</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Al Fahidi?</h4><p>Al Fahidi is ideal for those drawn to culture, history, and authenticity rather than modern luxury. It offers a rare opportunity to live or invest within one of Dubai’s most preserved heritage districts, surrounded by art, tradition, and the rhythm of old Dubai. For creatives, cultural enthusiasts, and niche investors, Al Fahidi provides character, identity, and timeless appeal that cannot be replicated elsewhere in the city.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'al-muteena'), 'ae', 'Al Muteena', 'A centrally located, affordable residential neighbourhood in Deira offering everyday convenience, strong connectivity, and a community-focused lifestyle.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Al Muteena is a well-established residential area located in the heart of Deira, Dubai’s historic commercial district. Known for its practicality and accessibility, the community appeals to residents who prioritise affordability, centrality, and daily convenience over luxury living. The area is predominantly residential, with a strong local feel and a long-standing expatriate population, making it one of Deira’s most lived-in neighbourhoods.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>Al Muteena offers a wide range of everyday dining options, from local eateries and cafés to casual international restaurants. While it is not a nightlife destination, residents benefit from proximity to Deira’s traditional souks, retail streets, and nearby shopping centres such as Al Ghurair Centre. The focus here is functionality rather than entertainment-led living.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>Outdoor options within Al Muteena itself are limited, but residents have access to nearby parks and public spaces across Deira. The neighbourhood suits residents with an urban lifestyle who spend more time working or commuting than engaging in outdoor recreation within the community.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>One of Al Muteena’s strongest advantages is connectivity. The area is served by multiple bus routes and nearby metro stations, offering easy access to Bur Dubai, Downtown Dubai, and Dubai International Airport. Major roads connect the neighbourhood efficiently to the wider city, making it highly practical for daily commuting.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>Al Muteena benefits from close proximity to schools, clinics, pharmacies, supermarkets, and essential services. Healthcare facilities and educational institutions are easily reachable within Deira and neighbouring communities. Everyday living here is straightforward, affordable, and well-supported by existing infrastructure.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>The area is dominated by low-rise and mid-rise apartment buildings, with a focus on functional layouts rather than premium finishes. Units are typically more spacious than newer high-density developments, appealing to residents seeking value for money.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>Al Muteena attracts consistent rental demand from long-term tenants, including working professionals and families seeking central, budget-conscious accommodation. Rental yields are generally stable, driven by affordability and location, though capital appreciation tends to be more modest compared to newer master-planned districts.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>Limited green spaces within the community</li><li>Older building stock compared to newer areas</li><li>Minimal lifestyle or entertainment attractions</li><li>Traffic congestion during peak commuting hours</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Al Muteena?</h4><p>Al Muteena is ideal for residents and investors focused on practicality, affordability, and central living. It offers a no-frills, community-driven environment with excellent access to transport, employment hubs, and everyday essentials. For those who value function over form and want to live close to the heart of old Dubai, Al Muteena remains a dependable option.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'al-safa'), 'ae', 'Al Safa', 'A centrally located, low-density residential district offering spacious villas, green spaces, and a quiet lifestyle just minutes from Dubai’s key business and leisure hubs.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Al Safa is a well-established residential district in central Dubai, positioned between Sheikh Zayed Road and Al Wasl Road. Known for its calm atmosphere and prime location, the area attracts long-term residents who want space, privacy, and easy access to the city without living in a high-rise or master-planned development. The community is split mainly between Al Safa 1 and Al Safa 2, both offering a suburban feel while remaining firmly within the city centre.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>Residents of Al Safa benefit from close proximity to City Walk, Boxpark, Downtown Dubai, and Jumeirah, where a wide range of cafés, restaurants, retail outlets, and entertainment venues are available. While Al Safa itself is primarily residential, everything from casual dining to premium lifestyle destinations is just a short drive away.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>Al Safa Park is one of the area’s standout features, offering large green spaces, jogging tracks, family areas, and recreational facilities. The neighbourhood is also ideal for walking and cycling, with wide streets and low traffic compared to denser districts. Beaches along Jumeirah are easily accessible within minutes.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>The district is extremely well connected, with direct access to Sheikh Zayed Road and Al Wasl Road. Metro stations along Sheikh Zayed Road are within short driving or walking distance depending on location, providing quick access to Downtown Dubai, DIFC, and Dubai Marina. The area is highly car-friendly while remaining centrally positioned.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>Al Safa is popular with families due to the number of international schools, nurseries, and learning centres nearby. Healthcare facilities, clinics, and hospitals are easily accessible across Jumeirah and Downtown. Daily essentials such as supermarkets, pharmacies, gyms, and service providers are readily available within the surrounding neighbourhoods.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>The area is dominated by low-rise residential villas, townhouses, and a limited number of boutique apartment buildings. Properties are known for generous plot sizes, private gardens, and quiet surroundings. New developments are rare, preserving the low-density character of the district.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>Al Safa appeals strongly to end-users and long-term renters seeking space and location rather than short-term returns. Rental demand is stable, driven by families and professionals working in central Dubai. Capital appreciation potential is supported by limited supply, central positioning, and ongoing demand for villa living near the city core.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>Limited availability of new or off-plan developments</li><li>Higher entry prices compared to outer suburban areas</li><li>Minimal retail and entertainment within the community itself</li><li>Villa-focused market may not suit apartment-only buyers</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Al Safa?</h4><p>Al Safa is ideal for residents who want calm, space, and centrality without sacrificing connectivity. It offers a rare combination of low-density living, green spaces, and proximity to Dubai’s most important districts. For families, professionals, and end-users seeking long-term stability in a prime location, Al Safa remains one of Dubai’s most dependable residential choices.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'al-satwa'), 'ae', 'Al Satwa', 'A centrally located, affordable urban neighbourhood offering low-rise housing, strong connectivity, and a community-driven lifestyle close to Dubai’s main business districts.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Al Satwa is one of Dubai’s most central and lived-in neighbourhoods, positioned just off Sheikh Zayed Road between Jumeirah, DIFC, and Downtown Dubai. Known for its authenticity and practicality, the district has long attracted residents who value location, affordability, and everyday convenience. While modest in appearance, Al Satwa benefits from a strategic city-centre position that keeps residents close to work, retail, and major transport routes.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>Al Satwa is rich in local dining, cafés, tailoring shops, and everyday retail outlets. The area is not a nightlife hub, but residents are minutes away from City Walk, DIFC, and Downtown Dubai for higher-end dining, entertainment, and social venues. Daily needs are easily met within the community itself.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>While Al Satwa has limited internal green spaces, residents benefit from nearby parks and public areas in Jumeirah and Downtown. The neighbourhood suits an urban lifestyle, with short commutes and walkable streets rather than destination-style outdoor living.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>Connectivity is one of Al Satwa’s strongest advantages. The district sits directly alongside Sheikh Zayed Road and Al Wasl Road, providing fast access to DIFC, Downtown Dubai, Jumeirah, and Business Bay. Metro stations along Sheikh Zayed Road are within walking or short driving distance, and the area is well served by bus routes.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>Al Satwa is supported by a wide range of nearby schools, nurseries, clinics, pharmacies, and hospitals across Jumeirah, Al Wasl, and Downtown. Supermarkets, gyms, salons, and essential services are spread throughout the neighbourhood, making day-to-day living practical and cost-effective for long-term residents.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>The area is characterised by low-rise apartment buildings, shared villas, and older residential stock. Homes here prioritise function and space over luxury finishes, making the district attractive to budget-conscious residents who want to live centrally.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>Al Satwa sees consistent long-term rental demand driven by its location and affordability. The area appeals primarily to end-users and long-term tenants rather than short-term rental investors. Capital appreciation tends to be gradual, supported by central positioning and redevelopment interest in surrounding districts.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>Older buildings with limited modern amenities</li><li>Limited green spaces within the community</li><li>Parking constraints in some streets</li><li>Busy atmosphere during peak hours</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Al Satwa?</h4><p>Al Satwa is ideal for residents who want to live close to Dubai’s commercial heart without paying premium prices. It offers strong connectivity, everyday convenience, and an authentic urban lifestyle. For tenants, professionals, and investors seeking central exposure with stable demand, Al Satwa remains a highly practical choice.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'al-sufouh'), 'ae', 'Al Sufouh', 'A low-density coastal district offering spacious villas, quiet residential living, and prime connectivity between Dubai Marina and the city’s main business hubs.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Al Sufouh is a strategically located residential district along Dubai’s coastline, positioned between Dubai Marina, Palm Jumeirah, and Al Barsha. Known for its calm atmosphere and low-rise character, the area attracts residents seeking privacy, space, and proximity to key employment and lifestyle zones. Unlike high-density waterfront districts, Al Sufouh maintains a subdued, residential feel while benefiting from one of the city’s most desirable locations.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>Al Sufouh itself is largely residential, with limited internal retail or nightlife. However, residents are minutes away from Dubai Marina, Mall of the Emirates, Palm Jumeirah, and Madinat Jumeirah, providing access to a wide range of dining, shopping, and entertainment options. The area is ideal for those who prefer quiet living with lifestyle destinations close by.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>The district benefits from proximity to public beaches, coastal walkways, and open spaces. Residents enjoy easy access to beachfront activities, jogging routes, and outdoor fitness along the coast. The low-traffic nature of the area also supports walking and cycling within residential streets.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>Al Sufouh is highly accessible via Sheikh Zayed Road and Al Khail Road, allowing efficient travel across Dubai. The Dubai Tram serves parts of the area, connecting residents to Dubai Marina and JBR, while metro stations are accessible within short driving distance. The district is well suited to car owners while remaining connected to public transport.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>Al Sufouh is particularly popular with families due to the presence of several well-regarded international schools and academic institutions nearby. Healthcare facilities, clinics, supermarkets, and daily services are readily available in neighbouring communities such as Al Barsha, The Greens, and Dubai Marina, supporting comfortable long-term living.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>The area is dominated by villas, large residential plots, and a limited number of low-rise apartment buildings. Properties typically offer generous layouts, private outdoor space, and quieter surroundings compared to nearby high-rise districts.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>Al Sufouh attracts stable long-term rental demand from families, professionals, and senior executives working in Media City, Internet City, and surrounding business hubs. Investment appeal is driven by limited supply, location strength, and consistent end-user demand rather than short-term rental yields.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>Limited retail and entertainment within the district itself</li><li>Few new or off-plan developments available</li><li>Villa-focused market may limit apartment options</li><li>Car dependency for most daily activities</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Al Sufouh?</h4><p>Al Sufouh is ideal for residents seeking coastal proximity, space, and privacy without the density of Marina or Palm living. It offers a balanced lifestyle with quiet residential streets, strong connectivity, and access to some of Dubai’s most important business and leisure destinations. For families and long-term end-users, it remains a highly desirable yet understated district.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'al-wasl'), 'ae', 'Al Wasl', 'A prime central residential district offering spacious villas, a refined urban lifestyle, and immediate access to Dubai’s key leisure and business destinations.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Al Wasl is a prestigious, well-established residential district in central Dubai, stretching along Al Wasl Road between Jumeirah, Downtown Dubai, and City Walk. Known for its understated elegance and low-density character, the area attracts long-term residents who value space, privacy, and proximity to the city’s most important hubs. Al Wasl balances a calm residential atmosphere with one of the most connected locations in Dubai.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>Residents enjoy close access to City Walk, Boxpark, Downtown Dubai, and Jumeirah, offering a wide range of dining, retail, cafés, and entertainment options. While Al Wasl itself remains primarily residential, premium lifestyle destinations are only minutes away, making it ideal for those who prefer quiet living with vibrant areas nearby.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>The district benefits from wide, walkable streets and proximity to Safa Park, Jumeirah Beach, and various outdoor fitness and leisure facilities. Cycling, jogging, and casual walking are common, supported by low traffic within residential pockets.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>Al Wasl is exceptionally well connected via Al Wasl Road, Sheikh Zayed Road, and Al Khail Road. Metro stations along Sheikh Zayed Road are easily accessible, providing fast links to DIFC, Downtown Dubai, and Dubai Marina. The area is highly car-friendly while remaining centrally positioned.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>Al Wasl is particularly attractive to families due to the availability of international schools, nurseries, and educational institutions nearby. Clinics, hospitals, supermarkets, gyms, and daily services are easily accessible across Jumeirah and Downtown, supporting comfortable long-term living.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>The area is dominated by large villas, townhouses, and a limited number of boutique low-rise apartment buildings. Properties typically offer generous plot sizes, private gardens, and quiet surroundings. New supply is limited, helping preserve the district’s residential character.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>Al Wasl appeals strongly to end-users and long-term tenants seeking prime central living. Rental demand is steady, driven by professionals and families working in nearby business districts. Capital appreciation potential is supported by limited supply, location strength, and continued demand for villa living close to the city centre.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>Higher entry prices compared to outer districts</li><li>Limited availability of new or off-plan developments</li><li>Minimal retail within the community itself</li><li>Villa-focused market may not suit all buyers</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Al Wasl?</h4><p>Al Wasl is ideal for residents seeking centrality, privacy, and long-term stability. It offers a refined residential lifestyle with excellent connectivity, spacious homes, and access to Dubai’s top destinations without the density of high-rise districts. For families and professionals prioritising location and livability, Al Wasl remains one of Dubai’s most desirable neighbourhoods.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'arabian-ranches-1'), 'ae', 'Arabian Ranches 1', 'A prestigious master-planned villa community offering spacious homes, green landscapes, and a family-focused lifestyle in a peaceful suburban setting.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Arabian Ranches 1 is one of Dubai’s most established and sought-after master-planned communities, developed by Emaar and located along Sheikh Mohammed Bin Zayed Road. Known for its landscaped streets, generous villa plots, and strong sense of community, the district has long been a benchmark for suburban family living in Dubai. It attracts end-users and long-term residents who prioritise space, privacy, and lifestyle over city-centre density.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>The community is supported by Arabian Ranches Retail Centre, offering supermarkets, cafés, restaurants, pharmacies, and essential services. For larger retail and entertainment destinations, residents are within easy driving distance of Mall of the Emirates, Cityland Mall, and Global Village. Nightlife is minimal, with the focus firmly on relaxed, residential living.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>Outdoor living is a core feature of Arabian Ranches 1. The community offers landscaped parks, walking paths, cycling routes, playgrounds, and sports courts. The Arabian Ranches Golf Club is a major highlight, providing an 18-hole championship course, clubhouse facilities, and dining options. The low-traffic environment makes it ideal for families and outdoor activity.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>Arabian Ranches 1 is primarily car-dependent, with direct access to Sheikh Mohammed Bin Zayed Road and Emirates Road, enabling efficient travel to key areas such as Dubai Marina, Downtown Dubai, and DIFC. While there is no metro access within the community, road connectivity is well established and reliable.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>The area is highly family-oriented, with several well-regarded schools and nurseries nearby, including JESS Arabian Ranches. Healthcare clinics, veterinary services, gyms, and daily essentials are conveniently located within or close to the community. Everything needed for long-term living is easily accessible without leaving the wider area.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>Arabian Ranches 1 consists almost entirely of villas and townhouses, ranging from 2-bedroom units to large family homes with private gardens. Architectural styles vary across sub-communities, offering a mix of Spanish, Arabic, and contemporary designs. Low-density planning and generous plot sizes define the area.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>The community enjoys strong long-term rental demand, particularly from families relocating to Dubai or upgrading from apartments. Arabian Ranches 1 is widely considered an end-user favourite, with stable yields and solid capital preservation supported by limited supply and consistent demand.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>Car dependency for daily commuting</li><li>Higher entry prices compared to newer outer communities</li><li>Limited nightlife and entertainment within the area</li><li>No direct metro or tram access</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Arabian Ranches 1?</h4><p>Arabian Ranches 1 is ideal for families and long-term residents seeking space, greenery, and a proven community environment. It offers a mature suburban lifestyle with excellent amenities, strong resale value, and a reputation for stability. For buyers prioritising livability, community, and long-term confidence, it remains one of Dubai’s safest residential choices.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'arabian-ranches-2'), 'ae', 'Arabian Ranches 2', 'A modern master-planned villa community offering contemporary homes, landscaped parks, and a premium family-focused lifestyle in a well-connected suburban setting.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Arabian Ranches 2 is a newer phase of the highly regarded Arabian Ranches development by Emaar, designed as an evolution of the original community with more contemporary architecture and upgraded infrastructure. Located along Al Qudra Road, the district attracts families and end-users seeking modern villas, open green spaces, and a secure, well-organised residential environment away from the city’s high-density areas.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>The community is supported by Ranches Souk, a central retail hub offering supermarkets, cafés, restaurants, pharmacies, fitness studios, and everyday services. For larger lifestyle and entertainment destinations, residents can access Mall of the Emirates, Cityland Mall, and Global Village within a short drive. Nightlife is limited, reinforcing the community’s calm, residential character.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>Outdoor living is a core part of Arabian Ranches 2. The area features landscaped parks, walking and cycling tracks, children’s play areas, sports courts, and community pools. The low-traffic, gated environment makes it particularly suitable for families and outdoor activities. Nearby equestrian facilities and open desert landscapes add to the lifestyle appeal.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>Arabian Ranches 2 is primarily car-dependent, with direct access to Al Qudra Road, Sheikh Mohammed Bin Zayed Road, and Emirates Road. This provides efficient connectivity to Dubai Marina, Downtown Dubai, and major business hubs. While there is no metro access within the community, road infrastructure is modern and well planned.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>The area is well served by international schools, nurseries, and learning centres within and around the Ranches and surrounding communities. Healthcare clinics, pharmacies, gyms, and essential services are conveniently accessible, making the district suitable for long-term family living without frequent trips into central Dubai.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>Arabian Ranches 2 consists mainly of modern villas and townhouses arranged in themed sub-communities. Homes typically feature contemporary layouts, private gardens, and access to shared community amenities. The overall density is low, with a strong emphasis on greenery and open space.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>The community benefits from strong long-term rental demand driven by families seeking newer homes within a reputable master-planned environment. Arabian Ranches 2 is primarily end-user focused, with stable yields and good capital preservation supported by developer reputation, infrastructure quality, and sustained demand.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>Car dependency for daily commuting</li><li>Limited nightlife and entertainment within the community</li><li>No direct metro or tram access</li><li>Pricing higher than some newer outer suburban developments</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Arabian Ranches 2?</h4><p>Arabian Ranches 2 is ideal for families and end-users seeking a modern suburban lifestyle within a proven Emaar community. It offers contemporary homes, excellent outdoor amenities, and a strong sense of neighbourhood while maintaining reliable connectivity to the wider city. For buyers prioritising comfort, space, and long-term stability, it remains one of Dubai’s most dependable villa communities.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'arabian-ranches-3'), 'ae', 'Arabian Ranches 3', 'A modern master-planned villa and townhouse community offering contemporary homes, family-friendly amenities, and green open spaces in a well-connected suburban location.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Arabian Ranches 3 is the newest phase of the Arabian Ranches master community by Emaar, designed with a more contemporary, lifestyle-led approach than its predecessors. Located along Sheikh Zayed Bin Hamdan Al Nahyan Street, the district focuses on modern architecture, active outdoor living, and community-centric planning. It attracts young families, first-time villa buyers, and end-users looking for a newer suburban environment with long-term growth potential.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>Arabian Ranches 3 is supported by community retail centres and neighbourhood conveniences, with supermarkets, cafés, and daily services integrated into the master plan. Larger retail and entertainment destinations such as Cityland Mall, Global Village, and Mall of the Emirates are accessible within a short drive. Nightlife is minimal, with the emphasis placed on relaxed, family-oriented living.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>Outdoor lifestyle is a defining feature of Arabian Ranches 3. The community includes extensive green spaces, central parks, cycling and jogging tracks, playgrounds, splash pads, sports courts, and recreational zones designed for all ages. Low-traffic internal roads and landscaped walkways make it particularly appealing for families with children.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>The district is primarily car-friendly, with direct access to major arterial roads including Sheikh Mohammed Bin Zayed Road and Emirates Road. This allows efficient commuting to key areas such as Downtown Dubai, Dubai Marina, and major employment hubs. Public transport options are currently limited, reflecting the area’s suburban planning.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>Arabian Ranches 3 benefits from proximity to several established schools, nurseries, and educational institutions within the wider Dubailand and Ranches areas. Clinics, fitness centres, supermarkets, and essential services are either within the community or easily accessible nearby, supporting comfortable long-term family living.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>The community is made up primarily of modern townhouses and villas, featuring contemporary designs, efficient layouts, private outdoor areas, and access to shared amenities. Compared to earlier Ranches phases, plot sizes are generally more compact, aligning with modern buyer preferences and affordability.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>Arabian Ranches 3 offers solid long-term rental appeal driven by demand for newer homes in reputable Emaar communities. It is particularly attractive to end-users and upgrade buyers, with capital appreciation potential supported by phased delivery, growing infrastructure, and continued demand for suburban family housing.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>Car dependency for most daily travel</li><li>Smaller plot sizes compared to Arabian Ranches 1</li><li>Limited nightlife and entertainment within the community</li><li>Ongoing development in surrounding areas</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Arabian Ranches 3?</h4><p>Arabian Ranches 3 is ideal for families and buyers seeking a modern, community-driven lifestyle in a trusted master-planned environment. It offers contemporary homes, extensive outdoor amenities, and strong long-term potential at a more accessible entry point than earlier Ranches phases. For those prioritising modern living, community, and future growth, it represents a compelling choice.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'bur-dubai'), 'ae', 'Bur Dubai', 'A historic central district offering affordable urban living, strong connectivity, and everyday convenience along Dubai Creek and the city’s original commercial core.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Bur Dubai is one of Dubai’s oldest and most established districts, forming the historic heart of the city alongside Deira. Situated along Dubai Creek, the area blends residential neighbourhoods, cultural landmarks, government buildings, and long-standing commercial zones. Bur Dubai appeals to residents and investors seeking centrality, affordability, and a deeply rooted urban environment rather than modern master-planned living.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>Bur Dubai is known for its diverse dining scene, ranging from traditional Middle Eastern and South Asian eateries to casual cafés and local restaurants. Shopping is centred around Meena Bazaar, Karama, and nearby retail streets, offering everyday goods rather than luxury brands. Nightlife is limited to hotel lounges and casual venues, with the focus remaining practical and community-oriented.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>The district benefits from proximity to Dubai Creek, offering waterfront walks, abra crossings, and scenic views of old Dubai. While internal green spaces are limited, nearby public parks and promenades provide accessible outdoor options. The area suits residents who enjoy walking, culture, and urban exploration.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>Bur Dubai is one of the city’s most connected districts, served by multiple Dubai Metro stations on both the Red and Green Lines. The area also benefits from extensive bus routes and abra services across the Creek, providing fast access to Deira. Major roads link Bur Dubai to Downtown Dubai, DIFC, and Sheikh Zayed Road.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>Bur Dubai offers excellent access to schools, clinics, hospitals, pharmacies, and government services. The district is particularly popular with long-term residents due to its affordability, availability of everyday services, and proximity to employment hubs. Supermarkets, gyms, and service providers are widely distributed throughout the area.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>The property landscape consists mainly of low-rise and mid-rise apartment buildings, with a small number of older villas and mixed-use developments. Homes prioritise functional layouts and value for money rather than luxury finishes.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>Bur Dubai has consistently strong long-term rental demand driven by affordability, central location, and access to transport. The area is investor-friendly for income-focused buyers, with stable yields and low vacancy rates, though capital appreciation tends to be steady rather than rapid.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>Older building stock in many areas</li><li>Limited green spaces within the district</li><li>Busy streets and congestion during peak hours</li><li>Limited luxury or modern lifestyle developments</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Bur Dubai?</h4><p>Bur Dubai is ideal for residents and investors who prioritise central living, connectivity, and everyday practicality. It offers a culturally rich environment, strong transport links, and dependable rental demand at accessible price points. For those seeking exposure to Dubai’s historic core with reliable long-term appeal, Bur Dubai remains a solid choice.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'business-bay'), 'ae', 'Business Bay', 'A dynamic central district offering high-rise living, canal-side residences, and strong rental demand next to Downtown Dubai and the city’s main business hubs.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Business Bay is one of Dubai’s most prominent mixed-use districts, positioned directly south of Downtown Dubai and built around the Dubai Water Canal. Designed as a commercial and residential hub, the area combines office towers, residential skyscrapers, hotels, and serviced apartments. Business Bay attracts professionals, investors, and end-users seeking central living with strong connectivity, modern infrastructure, and proximity to Dubai’s financial and lifestyle centres.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>Business Bay offers a growing selection of restaurants, cafés, lounges, and hotel dining options, particularly along the canal and within mixed-use towers. Residents are minutes from Downtown Dubai, Dubai Mall, and City Walk, providing access to premium shopping, nightlife, and entertainment. The lifestyle here is modern, fast-paced, and urban.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>The Dubai Water Canal promenade is a key lifestyle feature, offering walking and jogging paths, waterfront views, and open-air spaces. While internal green spaces are limited compared to suburban communities, the canal-side environment provides an appealing outdoor setting for fitness and leisure.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>Business Bay is extremely well connected via Sheikh Zayed Road and Al Khail Road. Business Bay Metro Station provides direct access to DIFC, Downtown Dubai, and Dubai Marina. The area is highly accessible for both private vehicles and public transport users, making it one of the most connected districts in the city.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>While Business Bay is more professional and investor-oriented than family-focused, residents benefit from easy access to schools, nurseries, and healthcare facilities in nearby areas such as Downtown Dubai, Al Wasl, and Jumeirah. Supermarkets, gyms, clinics, and daily services are widely available within the district’s residential and mixed-use towers.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>The district is dominated by high-rise residential towers offering apartments, serviced residences, and branded living options. Units range from studios to large penthouses, with many properties offering canal or skyline views. Buildings often include shared amenities such as gyms, pools, and concierge services.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>Business Bay is considered one of Dubai’s strongest areas for rental demand, driven by its central location and professional tenant base. The area performs well for both long-term and short-term rentals, making it attractive to investors. Capital appreciation potential is supported by ongoing infrastructure improvements and proximity to Downtown Dubai.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>Traffic congestion during peak business hours</li><li>High-density living with limited green spaces</li><li>Service charges can be higher in newer towers</li><li>Ongoing construction in certain pockets</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Business Bay?</h4><p>Business Bay is ideal for professionals and investors seeking centrality, connectivity, and modern urban living. It offers strong rental performance, waterfront lifestyle elements, and immediate access to Dubai’s most important commercial and leisure districts. For buyers prioritising convenience, yield potential, and city-centre exposure, Business Bay remains one of Dubai’s most compelling districts.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'deira'), 'ae', 'Deira', 'A historic, centrally located district offering affordable urban living, strong rental demand, and excellent connectivity along Dubai Creek and the city’s original commercial centre.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Deira is one of Dubai’s oldest and most culturally significant districts, forming the original commercial heart of the city along Dubai Creek. Known for its dense urban fabric, traditional markets, and diverse population, Deira blends residential neighbourhoods with busy retail and trading zones. The area appeals to residents and investors prioritising affordability, centrality, and everyday convenience over modern master-planned living.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>Deira is famous for its traditional souks, including the Gold Souk, Spice Souk, and Perfume Souk, alongside shopping centres such as City Centre Deira and Al Ghurair Centre. Dining options are extensive and diverse, ranging from local eateries to international cuisine. Nightlife is limited and largely hotel-based, with the focus remaining practical rather than lifestyle-led.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>Outdoor recreation in Deira centres around the Dubai Creek waterfront, offering promenades, abra crossings, and scenic views. While green spaces within the district are limited, nearby parks and public spaces provide accessible outdoor options. The area suits an urban, walkable lifestyle rather than recreational living.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>Deira is one of the best-connected districts in Dubai, served by multiple metro stations on both the Red and Green Lines. Extensive bus routes, abra services across the Creek, and proximity to Dubai International Airport make it a major transport hub. Major roads connect Deira efficiently to Downtown Dubai, Bur Dubai, and Sharjah.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>Deira offers strong access to schools, clinics, hospitals, pharmacies, and government services, making it popular with long-term residents. Everyday essentials such as supermarkets, gyms, salons, and service providers are widely available. Its affordability and infrastructure support stable, long-term living rather than short-term lifestyle demand.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>The district is dominated by low-rise and mid-rise apartment buildings, mixed-use developments, and a limited number of older villas. Properties focus on functional layouts and value for money rather than luxury finishes, with many buildings catering to long-term tenants.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>Deira benefits from consistently high rental demand driven by affordability, central location, and proximity to employment hubs. It is particularly attractive to income-focused investors seeking stable yields. Capital appreciation tends to be steady rather than rapid, reflecting the area’s maturity and density.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>Older building stock in many areas</li><li>High-density living and busy streets</li><li>Limited green spaces</li><li>Traffic congestion during peak hours</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Deira?</h4><p>Deira is ideal for residents and investors seeking central exposure at accessible price points. It offers excellent connectivity, dependable rental demand, and a culturally rich environment rooted in Dubai’s trading history. For those prioritising function, yield stability, and long-term demand over modern luxury, Deira remains a reliable and proven district.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'discovery-gardens'), 'ae', 'Discovery Gardens', 'An affordable, garden-style residential community offering low-rise apartments, green spaces, and excellent metro connectivity near Dubai Marina and Jebel Ali.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Discovery Gardens is a well-established residential district developed by Nakheel, located between Sheikh Zayed Road and Sheikh Mohammed Bin Zayed Road, close to Jebel Ali and Ibn Battuta Mall. Designed as a garden-style neighbourhood, the community is characterised by low-rise apartment clusters surrounded by landscaped green spaces. It appeals strongly to budget-conscious residents, young professionals, and investors seeking affordability with strong transport links.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>Residents benefit from proximity to Ibn Battuta Mall, one of Dubai’s largest shopping centres, offering extensive retail, dining, entertainment, and everyday services. Within the community, there are supermarkets, cafés, pharmacies, and local retail outlets catering to daily needs. Nightlife is minimal, with residents typically visiting nearby Marina or JBR for entertainment.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>Green spaces are a defining feature of Discovery Gardens. Landscaped gardens, open courtyards, and walking paths are spread throughout the community, creating a more relaxed and walkable environment compared to dense high-rise districts. The layout encourages outdoor movement and casual recreation.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>Discovery Gardens is highly accessible via the Dubai Metro Route 2020 line, with Discovery Gardens Metro Station providing direct connectivity to Dubai Marina, Business Bay, Downtown Dubai, and Expo City. Road access via Sheikh Zayed Road ensures efficient commuting, making it one of the most transport-friendly affordable communities in Dubai.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>The area offers convenient access to schools, nurseries, clinics, and hospitals in neighbouring communities such as Al Furjan, Jebel Ali, and The Gardens. Supermarkets, gyms, salons, and essential services are widely available within or near the community, supporting practical long-term living.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>Discovery Gardens consists almost entirely of low-rise apartment buildings, offering studios, one-bedroom, and two-bedroom units. Apartments are known for functional layouts and competitive pricing, with many units offering larger internal space compared to newer developments.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>The community is considered an investor favourite due to its affordability, metro access, and consistently high rental demand. Both long-term and short-term rentals perform well, particularly following infrastructure upgrades and metro connectivity. Capital appreciation is moderate but stable, supported by location and demand.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>Older building stock compared to newer communities</li><li>Limited luxury amenities within the district</li><li>Parking availability can vary by cluster</li><li>Community appearance is more functional than premium</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Discovery Gardens?</h4><p>Discovery Gardens is ideal for residents and investors seeking affordability without sacrificing connectivity. It offers a practical, green, and well-connected living environment with strong rental demand and access to key employment hubs. For value-driven buyers and tenants, it remains one of Dubai’s most reliable residential options.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'downtown-dubai'), 'ae', 'Downtown Dubai', 'Dubai’s most iconic city-centre district offering luxury high-rise living, world-class amenities, and unmatched access to shopping, business, and entertainment.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Downtown Dubai is the city’s most recognisable and prestigious urban district, home to global landmarks such as the Burj Khalifa and Dubai Mall. Developed by Emaar, the area is designed as a high-density, luxury city centre combining residential towers, commercial spaces, hospitality, and entertainment. Downtown attracts professionals, high-net-worth individuals, and investors seeking prime location, brand value, and strong demand.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>Downtown Dubai offers one of the most comprehensive lifestyle experiences in the region. Dubai Mall provides world-class retail, dining, cinemas, and entertainment, while surrounding boulevards feature fine dining restaurants, cafés, lounges, and luxury hotels. Nightlife is vibrant and upscale, with rooftop lounges, hotel bars, and premium venues throughout the district.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>The area features landscaped boulevards, pedestrian-friendly walkways, and access to the Dubai Fountain promenade. While traditional parks are limited, the district is highly walkable and visually engaging, encouraging outdoor movement through urban design rather than green space.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>Downtown Dubai is exceptionally well connected via Sheikh Zayed Road and Al Khail Road. Burj Khalifa Dubai Mall Metro Station provides direct access to the Red Line, connecting the district to DIFC, Business Bay, Dubai Marina, and Dubai International Airport. The area is both car-friendly and public-transport accessible.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>Although Downtown is not primarily family-oriented, residents benefit from proximity to international schools, nurseries, and healthcare facilities in Business Bay, Al Wasl, and Jumeirah. Supermarkets, gyms, clinics, and concierge-level services are widely available within residential towers, supporting premium everyday living.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>The district is dominated by high-rise luxury apartment towers, serviced residences, and branded residential developments. Units range from studios to expansive penthouses, many offering Burj Khalifa, fountain, or skyline views. Buildings typically include pools, gyms, concierge services, and high-end shared amenities.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>Downtown Dubai is one of the strongest-performing districts for rental demand, particularly for short-term and executive rentals. It attracts both end-users and investors, with premium pricing supported by global recognition, tourism demand, and limited comparable alternatives. Capital appreciation potential remains strong due to location and brand value.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>High entry prices compared to most districts</li><li>High service charges in luxury towers</li><li>Traffic congestion during peak tourist hours</li><li>Limited green spaces for families</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Downtown Dubai?</h4><p>Downtown Dubai is ideal for buyers seeking prestige, convenience, and a world-class urban lifestyle. It offers unmatched access to shopping, dining, and business hubs, along with strong rental demand and international appeal. For investors and end-users prioritising location, status, and liquidity, Downtown Dubai remains one of the city’s most compelling districts.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'dubai-festival-city'), 'ae', 'Dubai Festival City', 'A waterfront master-planned community offering modern residences, retail-led living, and strong connectivity near Dubai Creek and the city’s main business districts.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Dubai Festival City is a large-scale, master-planned waterfront district developed along Dubai Creek, close to Dubai International Airport and the city centre. The community blends residential living with retail, hospitality, and commercial spaces, anchored by one of Dubai’s most popular shopping destinations. It attracts families, professionals, and investors seeking a balanced lifestyle with water views, strong infrastructure, and everyday convenience.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>Dubai Festival City Mall is the heart of the community, offering a wide range of retail outlets, supermarkets, restaurants, cafés, cinemas, and entertainment options. The waterfront promenade hosts regular dining and leisure activity, including the IMAGINE fountain and light shows. Nightlife is relaxed and lifestyle-oriented, centred around restaurants and hotel lounges rather than clubs.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>The community features a well-developed waterfront promenade with walking and jogging paths, open spaces, and scenic Creek views. Residents enjoy an outdoor lifestyle supported by landscaped areas, fitness facilities, and family-friendly public spaces. While beaches are not within the community, waterfront access provides a strong lifestyle alternative.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>Dubai Festival City benefits from excellent road connectivity via Al Rebat Street and Sheikh Rashid Road, offering fast access to Downtown Dubai, Business Bay, and Dubai International Airport. The area is highly car-friendly, with public transport connections available via nearby metro stations and bus routes.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>The district is well suited for families, with schools, nurseries, clinics, and hospitals located within or close to the community. Supermarkets, gyms, pharmacies, and daily services are easily accessible, often within walking distance for residents living near the mall and central zones.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>Dubai Festival City offers a mix of high-rise apartment towers, waterfront residences, and serviced living options. Units range from studios to large family-sized apartments, many with Creek or skyline views. Buildings typically include shared amenities such as pools, gyms, concierge services, and retail access.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>The area benefits from steady rental demand driven by its integrated lifestyle, proximity to the airport, and central location. It appeals to both long-term tenants and professionals working in nearby business districts. Capital appreciation potential is supported by limited waterfront supply and continued infrastructure investment.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>Limited nightlife compared to Downtown or Marina</li><li>Car dependency for commuting outside the district</li><li>Service charges can be higher in waterfront towers</li><li>Less suitable for buyers seeking villa-only communities</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Dubai Festival City?</h4><p>Dubai Festival City is ideal for residents and investors seeking waterfront living with everyday convenience. It offers a well-rounded lifestyle combining retail, dining, and residential comfort in a centrally located, master-planned environment. For buyers prioritising accessibility, views, and long-term livability, it remains a strong and reliable choice.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'dubai-hills'), 'ae', 'Dubai Hills', 'A premium master-planned community offering villas and apartments, expansive green spaces, and a family-focused lifestyle centred around a championship golf course.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Dubai Hills Estate is one of Dubai’s flagship master-planned developments by Emaar, strategically located between Downtown Dubai and Dubai Marina. Designed as a self-contained city within the city, it combines residential neighbourhoods, retail, education, healthcare, and leisure around a central golf course and large parklands. The community attracts families, professionals, and investors seeking long-term livability, greenery, and strong infrastructure in a prime location.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>Dubai Hills Mall serves as the main retail and lifestyle hub, offering a wide range of shops, restaurants, cafés, entertainment options, and everyday services. Dining within the community is relaxed and family-oriented, with additional lifestyle destinations easily accessible in nearby areas such as Downtown Dubai, City Walk, and Mall of the Emirates. Nightlife is minimal and understated.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>Outdoor living is a defining feature of Dubai Hills. The community includes Dubai Hills Park, one of the city’s largest residential parks, featuring walking and cycling tracks, playgrounds, sports courts, and open lawns. The championship golf course, landscaped boulevards, and low-traffic streets support an active, outdoor-focused lifestyle.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>Dubai Hills Estate is well connected via Al Khail Road and Sheikh Mohammed Bin Zayed Road, providing efficient access to major business districts across Dubai. The community is car-friendly, with future public transport connectivity planned as the area continues to mature.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>Dubai Hills is highly family-oriented, offering international schools, nurseries, and higher education options within or near the community. King’s College Hospital London is located inside Dubai Hills, providing premium healthcare access. Supermarkets, gyms, clinics, and daily services are integrated throughout the development, supporting seamless long-term living.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>The community offers a diverse mix of property types, including luxury villas, townhouses, and mid- to high-rise apartment buildings. Homes range from entry-level apartments to large golf-course-facing villas, catering to a wide buyer spectrum. Architectural styles are modern, with a focus on space, light, and community views.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>Dubai Hills Estate is one of Dubai’s strongest-performing areas for long-term rental demand, driven by family appeal, developer reputation, and integrated amenities. It is popular with both end-users and investors, offering solid yields and strong capital appreciation potential as the community continues to mature.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>Premium pricing compared to older suburban areas</li><li>Ongoing development in certain phases</li><li>Car dependency for most daily travel</li><li>Higher entry prices for villa segments</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Dubai Hills?</h4><p>Dubai Hills is ideal for families and long-term residents seeking green space, quality infrastructure, and a balanced lifestyle in a central location. It offers one of Dubai’s most complete master-planned environments, combining nature, education, healthcare, and retail. For buyers prioritising livability, stability, and future growth, Dubai Hills Estate stands out as a top-tier choice.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'dubai-international-financial-centre'), 'ae', 'Dubai International Financial Centre', 'A prestigious city-centre financial district offering luxury high-rise living, world-class dining, and unrivalled access to Dubai’s business and lifestyle hubs.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Dubai International Financial Centre (DIFC) is a globally recognised financial free zone and one of Dubai’s most prestigious urban districts. Located between Downtown Dubai and Sheikh Zayed Road, DIFC blends Grade A office space with high-end residential towers, art venues, and fine dining. The district attracts senior professionals, executives, and investors seeking prime central living within a refined, international business environment.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>DIFC is one of Dubai’s top culinary destinations, home to award-winning restaurants, upscale cafés, and exclusive lounges. Gate Avenue offers boutique retail, galleries, and lifestyle services, while Downtown Dubai and City Walk are minutes away for expanded shopping and entertainment. Nightlife is sophisticated and premium, centred around hotel lounges and fine-dining venues.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>While green spaces are limited, DIFC features landscaped walkways, art-filled public spaces, and pedestrian-friendly boulevards. Residents also benefit from proximity to Downtown promenades and nearby fitness clubs, studios, and wellness centres.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>DIFC offers exceptional connectivity via Sheikh Zayed Road and Al Khail Road. Financial Centre Metro Station provides direct Red Line access to Downtown Dubai, Business Bay, Dubai Marina, and Dubai International Airport. The district is highly walkable and well suited to both car owners and public transport users.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>DIFC is not primarily family-oriented, but residents have access to international schools, nurseries, and healthcare facilities in nearby areas such as Downtown Dubai, Jumeirah, and Al Wasl. Supermarkets, clinics, gyms, and concierge-level services are widely available within residential towers, supporting premium everyday living.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>The district is dominated by high-rise luxury residential towers offering apartments, serviced residences, and branded living options. Units range from studios to large penthouses, often featuring skyline or Burj Khalifa views. Buildings typically include pools, gyms, concierge services, and private parking.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>DIFC is one of Dubai’s strongest locations for executive and professional rental demand. The area performs well for long-term rentals and select short-term corporate lets. Capital appreciation potential is supported by limited residential supply, global business appeal, and its position as a financial hub.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>High entry prices compared to most districts</li><li>Premium service charges in luxury towers</li><li>Limited green spaces</li><li>Not ideal for suburban or family-focused living</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose DIFC?</h4><p>DIFC is ideal for professionals and investors seeking prestige, connectivity, and a refined urban lifestyle. It offers unmatched access to Dubai’s financial core, top-tier dining, and central business districts. For buyers prioritising location, status, and strong rental demand, DIFC remains one of the city’s most exclusive and resilient markets.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'dubai-investments-park'), 'ae', 'Dubai Investments Park', 'A large-scale mixed-use district offering affordable residential living, strong industrial connectivity, and practical housing options in a well-connected suburban location.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Dubai Investments Park (DIP) is a master-planned mixed-use district in southwest Dubai, designed to integrate residential, commercial, and industrial zones within one large development. The area is divided into multiple sectors, with dedicated residential clusters separated from business and industrial activity. DIP appeals to residents and investors seeking affordability, space, and proximity to employment hubs, particularly in logistics, manufacturing, and commercial sectors.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>Lifestyle amenities within DIP are functional and convenience-led, including supermarkets, local retail outlets, cafés, and casual dining options. For larger shopping and entertainment destinations, residents typically travel to Ibn Battuta Mall, Festival Plaza, or Dubai Marina. Nightlife is minimal, aligning with the area’s practical, residential focus.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>The residential zones include landscaped areas, walking paths, and community parks, providing basic outdoor spaces for residents. While not lifestyle-driven in nature, the low-density layout supports casual outdoor activity and family-friendly movement within residential clusters.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>Dubai Investments Park benefits from strong road connectivity via Sheikh Mohammed Bin Zayed Road, Emirates Road, and Expo Road. The Dubai Metro Route 2020 serves the area through Dubai Investment Park Metro Station, offering direct access to Expo City, Jebel Ali, and central Dubai. The district is highly car-friendly and well suited to commuters.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>The area supports long-term living with access to schools, nurseries, clinics, pharmacies, and essential services within DIP and nearby communities. Healthcare facilities and educational institutions are easily reachable by car, making the area suitable for families and working professionals seeking value-driven accommodation.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>Dubai Investments Park offers a mix of low-rise apartment buildings, residential complexes, and villa communities in selected zones. Apartments dominate the market, typically offering spacious layouts at competitive price points compared to more central districts.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>DIP attracts steady long-term rental demand driven by affordability and proximity to employment zones. It is popular among tenants working in nearby industrial, logistics, and commercial areas. For investors, the district offers stable yields and lower entry prices, though capital appreciation tends to be gradual rather than rapid.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>Industrial and commercial activity in nearby zones</li><li>Limited lifestyle and entertainment offerings</li><li>Car dependency for most daily activities</li><li>Less premium feel compared to central districts</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Dubai Investments Park?</h4><p>Dubai Investments Park is ideal for residents and investors prioritising affordability, space, and connectivity to employment hubs. It offers practical housing options, metro access, and dependable rental demand at accessible price points. For value-focused buyers and long-term tenants, DIP remains a functional and reliable choice.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'dubai-marina'), 'ae', 'Dubai Marina', 'Dubai Marina is a man-made waterfront district in Dubai, built along a 3 km stretch of Persian Gulf shoreline.', '<h4 id="">1. Community overview</h4><ul id=""><li id="">Dubai Marina is a man-made waterfront district in Dubai, built along a 3 km stretch of Persian Gulf shoreline. It was developed to accommodate over 120,000 people when complete. It is located along Sheikh Zayed Road (E11), between Jumeirah Lake Towers/Free Zone area and the beachfront community of Jumeirah Beach Residence (JBR). Developed by Emaar Properties among others, it is designed for high-rise living with premium amenities. Demographically, the neighbourhood is cosmopolitan, popular with professionals, expatriates and investors.</li></ul><p id="">‍</p><figure id="" class="w-richtext-figure-type-image w-richtext-align-normal" data-rt-type="image" data-rt-align="normal"><div id=""><img id="" alt="__wf_reserved_inherit" src="https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/68ada6f5bc65af392b69f8d7/6908aefb7c72df87c4dfb978_dubai-puerto-deportivo-hd.jpg" width="auto" height="auto" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption id="">Dubai marina</figcaption></figure><p id="">‍</p><h4 id="">2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p id=""><strong id="">Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong></p><ul id=""><li id="">The stretch of the Marina Walk (about 7 km long) is lined with cafés, restaurants, boutiques and waterfront ambience.</li><li id="">Key hubs: Dubai Marina Mall with retail/f&amp;b; Pier 7 a seven-floor dining destination with multiple restaurants &amp; terraces overlooking the marina.</li><li id="">Waterfront lifestyle: cafés, yachts, promenades and beach nearby (via JBR) make it very appealing for leisure.</li></ul><p id=""><strong id="">Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong></p><ul id=""><li id="">You can take a stroll or cycle along the Marina Walk; jogging and waterfront views are strong features.</li><li id="">Water-based activities: yacht cruises, boat trips, water-sports via the marina and adjacent beach.</li><li id="">Beach access: though the marina itself isn’t a typical public beach, JBR is an easy walk or tram ride away for swimming, sunbathing and sand.</li></ul><p id=""><strong id="">Transport &amp; connectivity</strong></p><ul id=""><li id="">Two metro stations serve the area (e.g., DMCC, Sobha Realty) and the Dubai Tram circulates between JBR and Marina.</li><li id="">Major road access via Sheikh Zayed Road (E11) → the airport, Downtown Dubai and business districts are reachable.</li></ul><p id="">‍</p><figure id="" class="w-richtext-figure-type-image w-richtext-align-normal" data-rt-type="image" data-rt-align="normal"><div id=""><img id="" alt="__wf_reserved_inherit" src="https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/68ada6f5bc65af392b69f8d7/6908af7618a16a610ebb0283_4P2A0331-scaled.jpg" width="auto" height="auto" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption id="">Marina Medical Center</figcaption></figure><p id="">‍</p><h4 id="">3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><ul id=""><li id="">Nurseries are available in-community (e.g., early years). Schools for older children tend to be in adjacent districts (within ~15 minutes'' drive)</li><li id="">Medical clinics and centres are present. For hospital-level care, nearby Al Barsha etc. provide options.</li><li id="">Grocery/retail: Several supermarkets (e.g., Waitrose in Marina Mall, Spinneys, Al Maya) serve residents.</li></ul><p id="">‍</p><figure id="" class="w-richtext-figure-type-image w-richtext-align-normal" data-rt-type="image" data-rt-align="normal"><div id=""><img id="" alt="__wf_reserved_inherit" src="https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/68ada6f5bc65af392b69f8d7/6908af8e7c72df87c4dfe602_emirates-international-school-1695815828.webp" width="auto" height="auto" loading="lazy"></div></figure><p id="">‍</p><h4 id="">4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p id=""><strong id="">Housing typologies</strong></p><ul id=""><li id="">Predominantly high-rise apartments (studios through to penthouses) with marina, city or gulf views.</li><li id="">Podium villas and townhouses are few (so luxury ground-level or villa homes are rarer).</li></ul><p id=""><strong id="">Investment / rental appeal</strong></p><ul id=""><li id="">Strong attraction for investors and tenants due to waterfront location, amenities, transport links.</li><li id="">Rent levels: For example, one-bedroom apartments start around AED 110,000/year in recent data.</li><li id="">Sale levels: Studios may begin from ~AED 560k; 1-bedroom from ~AED 999k (figures indicative).</li></ul><h4 id="">5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul id=""><li id="">Because of its popularity and centrality, pricing is elevated relative to less premium neighbourhoods.</li><li id="">Parking and road congestion can be issues especially in peak hours.</li><li id="">The majority of properties are high-rise; for families seeking large villas, selection is limited in Marina.</li><li id="">Beach access is very good (via JBR) but the marina waterway itself is not always suitable for swimming.</li></ul><h4 id="">6. Why choose Dubai Marina?</h4><ul id=""><li id="">Iconic waterfront living in one of Dubai’s most recognised districts views, yachts, skyline.</li><li id="">Excellent lifestyle offering: cafés, dining, shopping, promenade, beach all close.</li><li id="">Solid connectivity: metro + tram + main roads make access to business districts, airport or island destinations quite practicable.</li><li id="">Investment friendly: high demand from ex-patriates and tenants for premium finishes and location.</li></ul><p id="">‍</p><blockquote id="">“Dubai Marina offers a cosmopolitan waterfront neighbourhood in Dubai where high-rise luxury apartments meet marina-front promenades, cafés, shopping and beach-side leisure. With top transport links, resort-style amenities and strong investor appeal, it represents a premium choice for those seeking vibrant, modern urban living by the water.”</blockquote><p id="">‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'dubai-media-city'), 'ae', 'Dubai Media City', 'A central business-focused district offering modern apartments, strong corporate demand, and excellent connectivity near Dubai Marina and Palm Jumeirah.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Dubai Media City is a prominent free-zone business district established as a regional hub for media, technology, and creative industries. Strategically located along Sheikh Zayed Road between Dubai Marina and Palm Jumeirah, the area blends commercial office towers with a limited but high-demand residential offering. It attracts professionals, executives, and investors seeking proximity to major business hubs in a modern, well-connected environment.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>While Dubai Media City is primarily business-oriented, it offers a solid selection of cafés, casual restaurants, and hotel dining options catering to professionals. For a broader lifestyle experience, residents are minutes from Dubai Marina, JBR, Palm Jumeirah, and Mall of the Emirates, providing access to premium dining, retail, and nightlife.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>The district benefits from landscaped public spaces, pedestrian walkways, and proximity to the coastline. Nearby beach access at Palm Jumeirah and Al Sufouh, along with Marina promenades, supports an active outdoor lifestyle. Fitness centres and wellness studios are widely available within and around the district.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>Dubai Media City is extremely well connected via Sheikh Zayed Road and Al Khail Road. The Dubai Tram runs through the area, linking it to Dubai Marina Metro Station and JBR. This makes the district highly accessible for both car owners and public transport users, particularly professionals commuting daily.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>Although not a traditional family-focused community, Dubai Media City benefits from proximity to schools, nurseries, and healthcare facilities in nearby areas such as The Greens, Al Sufouh, and Dubai Marina. Supermarkets, clinics, gyms, and everyday services are easily accessible within neighbouring residential districts.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>Residential options in Dubai Media City consist mainly of high-rise apartment buildings and serviced residences integrated into mixed-use towers. Units range from studios to larger executive apartments, often offering city or partial sea views. The residential supply is limited compared to surrounding communities.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>Dubai Media City performs strongly for long-term rentals due to its professional tenant base and proximity to major employment hubs such as Internet City, Knowledge Park, and Marina. Demand is driven by executives and corporate tenants, making it attractive for investors seeking stable, income-focused returns rather than mass-market appreciation.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>Limited residential inventory</li><li>Business-oriented environment rather than community living</li><li>Higher service charges in mixed-use towers</li><li>Less suitable for families seeking suburban amenities</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Dubai Media City?</h4><p>Dubai Media City is ideal for professionals and investors prioritising location, connectivity, and proximity to major business hubs. It offers a modern, efficient lifestyle with strong rental demand and quick access to Dubai’s key leisure destinations. For buyers seeking corporate-led rental stability in a central location, it remains a compelling option.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'dubai-parks-and-resorts'), 'ae', 'Dubai Parks and Resorts', 'A large-scale entertainment and lifestyle district offering affordable residential options, leisure-driven living, and strong connectivity near Expo City and Jebel Ali.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Dubai Parks and Resorts is a purpose-built entertainment and lifestyle destination located along Sheikh Zayed Road between Dubai and Abu Dhabi. The district is home to multiple theme parks, hotels, and leisure attractions, supported by nearby residential developments. It appeals to value-driven residents, hospitality professionals, and investors seeking affordable entry points close to major tourism and employment zones.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>Riverland Dubai forms the lifestyle heart of the district, offering restaurants, cafés, themed dining, and entertainment venues in a pedestrian-friendly setting. Additional dining and retail options are available within hotels and surrounding developments. Nightlife is casual and family-oriented rather than club-focused.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>The area is designed around leisure and entertainment, with open-air promenades, attractions, and recreational spaces. Residents benefit from access to theme parks, walking areas, and nearby open desert landscapes. While traditional parks are limited, the environment supports a relaxed, destination-style outdoor lifestyle.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>Dubai Parks and Resorts benefits from direct access to Sheikh Zayed Road, providing efficient connectivity to Dubai Marina, Jebel Ali, and Abu Dhabi. The Dubai Metro Route 2020 serves nearby areas, with Expo City station accessible by short drive. The district is highly car-friendly and suited to commuters working in surrounding zones.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>Everyday services are available within nearby communities such as Dubai South and Jebel Ali, including schools, nurseries, clinics, supermarkets, and healthcare facilities. The area supports practical long-term living for residents working locally, though many services require short drives.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>Residential options near Dubai Parks and Resorts primarily consist of low-rise apartment buildings, townhouses, and emerging residential clusters. Properties are typically priced competitively, offering functional layouts and value-oriented living compared to central Dubai districts.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>The area offers potential for long-term rental demand driven by hospitality, logistics, and Expo-related employment. It also attracts investors seeking lower entry prices with exposure to tourism-led infrastructure. Capital appreciation is gradual and linked to wider development and population growth in Dubai South.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>Distance from central Dubai districts</li><li>Limited mature community infrastructure</li><li>Car dependency for daily activities</li><li>Demand influenced by tourism cycles</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Dubai Parks and Resorts?</h4><p>Dubai Parks and Resorts is ideal for buyers and tenants seeking affordability, space, and proximity to emerging growth zones. It offers a unique leisure-led environment with long-term upside tied to tourism and infrastructure development. For investors and end-users focused on value and future potential rather than immediate lifestyle density, it remains a practical option.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'dubai-silicon-oasis'), 'ae', 'Dubai Silicon Oasis', 'A self-contained, tech-focused district offering affordable apartments, strong rental demand, and everyday convenience in a well-connected suburban location.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Dubai Silicon Oasis (DSO) is a government-owned, master-planned technology free zone designed to integrate residential living with business, education, and innovation. Located along Sheikh Mohammed Bin Zayed Road, the district combines tech parks, offices, universities, and residential neighbourhoods into a single ecosystem. It attracts professionals, students, families, and investors looking for value-driven living with consistent demand.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>DSO offers a wide range of everyday amenities including supermarkets, cafés, casual restaurants, and local retail outlets spread throughout the community. Souq Extra and community retail centres cover daily needs, while larger malls such as Cityland Mall and Festival City are a short drive away. Nightlife is limited, aligning with the area’s practical, residential focus.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>The community features landscaped parks, walking paths, and open spaces integrated between residential clusters. Gyms, sports courts, and fitness facilities are widely available, supporting a balanced, everyday lifestyle rather than destination-led recreation.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>Dubai Silicon Oasis benefits from excellent road connectivity via Sheikh Mohammed Bin Zayed Road, Al Ain Road, and Emirates Road. The district is car-friendly and well connected to major employment hubs across Dubai. While metro access is not currently available within the community, public transport links continue to improve as the area matures.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>DSO is well suited for long-term living, offering schools, nurseries, universities, clinics, pharmacies, and healthcare facilities within or near the community. Supermarkets, gyms, salons, and essential services are widely distributed, making daily life convenient for families and professionals.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>The district consists primarily of mid-rise and high-rise apartment buildings offering studios, one-bedroom, and two-bedroom units. Apartments are known for functional layouts, competitive pricing, and modern finishes relative to their price point. A small number of villa communities exist on the outskirts of the area.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>Dubai Silicon Oasis is considered an investor favourite due to its affordability, strong tenant base, and consistent long-term rental demand driven by professionals and students. Rental yields are typically stable, and entry prices remain accessible compared to central Dubai districts. Capital appreciation is gradual and supported by infrastructure growth.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>No direct metro access at present</li><li>Limited nightlife and entertainment</li><li>Car dependency for most commuting</li><li>Less premium feel than central districts</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Dubai Silicon Oasis?</h4><p>Dubai Silicon Oasis is ideal for buyers and tenants seeking affordability, practicality, and dependable rental demand. It offers a self-sufficient living environment with strong employment proximity and everyday convenience. For investors and end-users focused on value, stability, and long-term usability, DSO remains one of Dubai’s most reliable suburban districts.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'emirates-hills'), 'ae', 'Emirates Hills', 'Dubai’s most exclusive gated villa community offering ultra-luxury homes, championship golf views, and complete privacy in a prime central location.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Emirates Hills is widely regarded as Dubai’s most prestigious residential enclave, often compared to Beverly Hills for its exclusivity and scale. Developed by Emaar, the community is fully gated and built around the Montgomerie Golf Course, featuring some of the largest and most valuable private villas in the city. It attracts ultra-high-net-worth individuals, senior executives, and international investors seeking privacy, security, and long-term capital preservation.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>Emirates Hills is intentionally residential and private, with no retail or nightlife inside the community. Residents rely on nearby lifestyle hubs such as Dubai Marina, Jumeirah Lake Towers, Mall of the Emirates, and Palm Jumeirah for dining, shopping, and entertainment. The emphasis is on discretion and exclusivity rather than activity within the neighbourhood.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>The Montgomerie Golf Course is the centrepiece of Emirates Hills, offering an 18-hole championship course, clubhouse, and landscaped fairways. The community also features lakes, tree-lined streets, and walking areas, creating a serene, resort-like environment. Private gardens and outdoor spaces form a major part of daily living.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>Emirates Hills benefits from excellent connectivity via Sheikh Zayed Road and Al Khail Road, providing quick access to Dubai Marina, DIFC, Downtown Dubai, and major business hubs. The area is entirely car-dependent, which aligns with its low-density, private nature.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>The community is popular with families due to proximity to top international schools, including those in Meadows, Springs, and Jumeirah. World-class healthcare facilities are easily accessible in nearby districts. Everyday services are located outside the gates, reinforcing the private residential focus of the area.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>Emirates Hills consists exclusively of large, custom-built luxury villas on expansive plots. Architectural styles vary widely, with homes featuring bespoke designs, private pools, landscaped gardens, and panoramic golf or lake views. There are no apartments or townhouses within the community.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>The area is primarily end-user focused, with limited rental activity due to high entry prices. Investment appeal lies in long-term capital appreciation, scarcity, and global prestige rather than yield. Emirates Hills consistently ranks among Dubai’s most valuable residential markets.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>Extremely high entry prices</li><li>No retail, dining, or services within the community</li><li>Fully car-dependent lifestyle</li><li>Limited liquidity compared to mass-market districts</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Emirates Hills?</h4><p>Emirates Hills is ideal for buyers seeking absolute privacy, security, and status. It offers unmatched exclusivity, expansive custom homes, and a proven track record of capital preservation. For ultra-high-net-worth individuals prioritising discretion, space, and long-term value, Emirates Hills stands at the top of Dubai’s residential hierarchy.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'garhoud'), 'ae', 'Garhoud', 'A centrally located residential district offering spacious villas and apartments, strong connectivity, and everyday convenience close to Dubai International Airport.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Garhoud (also known as Al Garhoud) is a long-established residential district in eastern Dubai, positioned between Deira, Dubai Festival City, and Dubai International Airport. Known for its low-rise character and central location, the area attracts long-term residents who value space, accessibility, and practicality. Garhoud combines residential calm with immediate access to major commercial, retail, and transport hubs.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>Garhoud offers a selection of local restaurants, cafés, and everyday retail outlets serving the community. Larger lifestyle destinations such as Dubai Festival City Mall, City Centre Deira, and nearby hotel dining venues provide expanded shopping and entertainment options. Nightlife is limited and primarily hotel-based, keeping the district residential in feel.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>While Garhoud is not known for large parks or destination recreation, its wide streets and quieter residential pockets support walking and everyday outdoor activity. Residents also benefit from proximity to waterfront promenades at Dubai Creek and recreational areas in Festival City.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>One of Garhoud’s biggest strengths is connectivity. The district is minutes from Dubai International Airport and offers easy access to Sheikh Rashid Road, Airport Road, and major arterial routes. GGICO and Airport Terminal metro stations on the Red Line provide fast connections to Downtown Dubai, DIFC, and Dubai Marina. The area is highly car-friendly while remaining well served by public transport.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>Garhoud is popular with families due to its proximity to reputable schools, nurseries, and educational institutions in Deira and surrounding areas. Healthcare access is strong, with hospitals, clinics, and pharmacies nearby. Supermarkets, gyms, salons, and essential services are widely available, supporting comfortable long-term living.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>The district features a mix of low-rise apartment buildings and spacious villas, many with private gardens and parking. Properties tend to offer larger layouts compared to newer developments, appealing to families and professionals seeking space over modern high-rise living.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>Garhoud benefits from steady long-term rental demand driven by its central location, airport proximity, and family appeal. It attracts airline staff, professionals, and long-term residents. Investment performance is typically stable and income-focused, with gradual capital appreciation rather than rapid growth.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>Older building stock in some areas</li><li>Limited nightlife and lifestyle attractions within the district</li><li>Aircraft noise in pockets close to the airport</li><li>Fewer new or off-plan developments</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Garhoud?</h4><p>Garhoud is ideal for residents seeking space, centrality, and everyday convenience without the density of newer districts. It offers strong connectivity, family-friendly living, and dependable rental demand in one of Dubai’s most accessible locations. For end-users and investors prioritising practicality and long-term stability, Garhoud remains a solid choice.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'hor-al-anz'), 'ae', 'Hor Al Anz', 'A centrally located Deira neighbourhood offering affordable apartment living, strong public transport access, and everyday convenience close to Dubai’s historic commercial core.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Hor Al Anz is a long-established residential district in Deira, located near Abu Hail and Al Mamzar. Known for its affordability and dense urban layout, the area attracts long-term residents, working professionals, and value-focused tenants who prioritise centrality and connectivity. The neighbourhood has a practical, lived-in feel, with residential buildings supported by extensive local retail and services.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>Hor Al Anz offers a wide range of local restaurants, cafés, bakeries, and convenience stores catering to everyday needs. Larger retail destinations such as Al Ghurair Centre and City Centre Deira are a short drive away. Nightlife is minimal and largely limited to nearby hotel lounges, keeping the area focused on residential living.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>While green spaces within Hor Al Anz are limited, residents benefit from proximity to Al Mamzar Beach Park and nearby public parks. The neighbourhood is walkable, with daily errands easily managed on foot, supporting a practical urban lifestyle.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>The district is well connected via Abu Hail and Al Qiyadah Metro Stations on the Green Line, along with extensive bus routes. Major roads provide easy access to Deira, Bur Dubai, and Sharjah, making Hor Al Anz a convenient base for commuters. Public transport accessibility is one of the area’s key strengths.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>Hor Al Anz is supported by nearby schools, nurseries, clinics, pharmacies, and hospitals across Deira and Abu Hail. Supermarkets, gyms, salons, and essential services are widely available within the neighbourhood, making it suitable for long-term, budget-conscious living.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>The area is dominated by low-rise and mid-rise apartment buildings offering studios and one- to two-bedroom units. Properties are functional and value-oriented, with limited luxury or new-build stock.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>Hor Al Anz benefits from consistently high long-term rental demand due to its affordability and transport connectivity. It appeals to income-focused investors seeking stable occupancy and reliable yields, though capital appreciation is typically gradual.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>High-density living</li><li>Older buildings in many parts of the district</li><li>Limited internal green spaces</li><li>Busy streets during peak hours</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Hor Al Anz?</h4><p>Hor Al Anz is ideal for residents and investors seeking central, affordable living with strong transport access. It offers dependable rental demand, everyday convenience, and proximity to Deira’s commercial zones. For value-driven buyers prioritising practicality over lifestyle branding, it remains a solid and reliable option.</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'international-city'), 'ae', 'International City', 'Dubai’s most exclusive gated villa community offering ultra-luxury homes, championship golf views, and complete privacy in a prime central location.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Emirates Hills is widely regarded as Dubai’s most prestigious residential enclave, often compared to Beverly Hills for its exclusivity and scale. Developed by Emaar, the community is fully gated and built around the Montgomerie Golf Course, featuring some of the largest and most valuable private villas in the city. It attracts ultra-high-net-worth individuals, senior executives, and international investors seeking privacy, security, and long-term capital preservation.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>Emirates Hills is intentionally residential and private, with no retail or nightlife inside the community. Residents rely on nearby lifestyle hubs such as Dubai Marina, Jumeirah Lake Towers, Mall of the Emirates, and Palm Jumeirah for dining, shopping, and entertainment. The emphasis is on discretion and exclusivity rather than activity within the neighbourhood.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>The Montgomerie Golf Course is the centrepiece of Emirates Hills, offering an 18-hole championship course, clubhouse, and landscaped fairways. The community also features lakes, tree-lined streets, and walking areas, creating a serene, resort-like environment. Private gardens and outdoor spaces form a major part of daily living.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>Emirates Hills benefits from excellent connectivity via Sheikh Zayed Road and Al Khail Road, providing quick access to Dubai Marina, DIFC, Downtown Dubai, and major business hubs. The area is entirely car-dependent, which aligns with its low-density, private nature.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>The community is popular with families due to proximity to top international schools, including those in Meadows, Springs, and Jumeirah. World-class healthcare facilities are easily accessible in nearby districts. Everyday services are located outside the gates, reinforcing the private residential focus of the area.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>Emirates Hills consists exclusively of large, custom-built luxury villas on expansive plots. Architectural styles vary widely, with homes featuring bespoke designs, private pools, landscaped gardens, and panoramic golf or lake views. There are no apartments or townhouses within the community.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>The area is primarily end-user focused, with limited rental activity due to high entry prices. Investment appeal lies in long-term capital appreciation, scarcity, and global prestige rather than yield. Emirates Hills consistently ranks among Dubai’s most valuable residential markets.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>Extremely high entry prices</li><li>No retail, dining, or services within the community</li><li>Fully car-dependent lifestyle</li><li>Limited liquidity compared to mass-market districts</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Emirates Hills?</h4><p>Emirates Hills is ideal for buyers seeking absolute privacy, security, and status. It offers unmatched exclusivity, expansive custom homes, and a proven track record of capital preservation. For ultra-high-net-worth individuals prioritising discretion, space, and long-term value, Emirates Hills stands at the top of Dubai’s residential hierarchy.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'jumeirah'), 'ae', 'Jumeirah', 'A prestigious coastal residential district offering spacious villas, beach access, and a refined lifestyle close to Dubai’s key business and leisure hubs.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Jumeirah is one of Dubai’s most established and desirable residential districts, stretching along the coastline between Downtown Dubai and Dubai Marina. Known for its low-rise character, beachfront proximity, and upscale villa living, the area has long attracted families, professionals, and high-net-worth residents. Jumeirah combines traditional charm with modern convenience, offering a calm residential atmosphere in a prime city location.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>Jumeirah offers a wide range of cafés, boutique restaurants, beach clubs, and casual dining spots spread across Jumeirah Road and surrounding streets. Popular lifestyle destinations such as La Mer, City Walk, and Boxpark are nearby, while Downtown Dubai provides premium shopping and entertainment. Nightlife is relaxed and refined, focused on beach clubs and hotel lounges rather than high-energy clubbing.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>Beach access is one of Jumeirah’s defining features, with multiple public beaches, jogging tracks, and waterfront promenades. The area encourages an outdoor lifestyle, with cycling paths, parks, and easy access to the sea. Jumeirah Beach Park and nearby open spaces add to the appeal for families and active residents.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>Jumeirah is well connected via Jumeirah Road, Al Wasl Road, and Sheikh Zayed Road, providing efficient access to Downtown Dubai, DIFC, and Dubai Marina. The area is primarily car-friendly, with public transport options available via bus routes and nearby metro stations in adjacent districts.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>Jumeirah is highly family-oriented, with access to top international schools, nurseries, and educational institutions. Healthcare facilities, clinics, and hospitals are widely available, including premium private medical centres. Supermarkets, gyms, salons, and daily services are spread throughout the district, supporting comfortable long-term living.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>The district is dominated by large villas, townhouses, and a limited number of low-rise apartment buildings. Properties typically feature private gardens, generous plot sizes, and quiet residential streets. New supply is limited, preserving the area’s low-density, premium character.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>Jumeirah appeals strongly to end-users and long-term tenants seeking beachfront proximity and space. Rental demand is stable, particularly among families and senior professionals. Investment appeal lies in long-term capital preservation and scarcity rather than high short-term yields.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>High entry prices compared to non-coastal districts</li><li>Limited availability of new or off-plan developments</li><li>Car dependency for daily commuting</li><li>Villa-focused market may not suit apartment-only buyers</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Jumeirah?</h4><p>Jumeirah is ideal for buyers seeking a premium coastal lifestyle with space, privacy, and long-term stability. It offers beach access, family-friendly living, and proximity to Dubai’s major destinations without the density of high-rise districts. For end-users prioritising quality of life and capital preservation, Jumeirah remains one of Dubai’s most timeless residential choices.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'jumeirah-beach-residence-jbr'), 'ae', 'Jumeirah Beach Residence (JBR)', 'A vibrant beachfront district offering high-rise living, direct beach access, and a lifestyle-driven environment next to Dubai Marina.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Jumeirah Beach Residence (JBR) is one of Dubai’s most recognisable waterfront districts, located along the Arabian Gulf and adjacent to Dubai Marina. Designed as a high-density beachfront community, JBR combines residential towers, hotels, retail, and entertainment along a lively promenade. The area attracts residents, tourists, and investors seeking beachside living with constant activity, strong demand, and iconic views.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>The Walk at JBR and The Beach at JBR form the heart of the community, offering a wide selection of restaurants, cafés, retail outlets, cinemas, and beachfront dining. The nightlife scene is active, with beach clubs, lounges, and hotel venues creating a lively atmosphere day and night.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>Direct beach access is a defining feature of JBR. Residents enjoy swimming, jogging along the promenade, outdoor gyms, water sports, and open-air leisure throughout the year. The beachfront setting supports an active, outdoor-focused lifestyle unmatched by most city districts.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>JBR is well connected via Sheikh Zayed Road and served by Dubai Tram, which links the community to Dubai Marina Metro Station and JLT. While the area is walkable internally, traffic congestion can occur during peak tourist periods. Public transport access remains strong for a beachfront location.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>JBR is not primarily family-suburban, but residents have access to schools, nurseries, and healthcare facilities in nearby communities such as Dubai Marina, JLT, and Al Sufouh. Supermarkets, gyms, pharmacies, and daily services are widely available within the towers and along the promenade.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>The district consists mainly of high-rise apartment towers offering studios, one- to four-bedroom apartments, and penthouses. Many units feature sea, Marina, or Ain Dubai views. Buildings typically include pools, gyms, concierge services, and direct access to retail or beachfront areas.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>JBR is one of Dubai’s strongest areas for short-term rental performance due to its beachfront location and tourist demand. Long-term rentals are also popular with professionals and lifestyle-focused tenants. Pricing is supported by limited beachfront supply and consistent international demand.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>Tourist-heavy environment</li><li>Traffic congestion during weekends and peak seasons</li><li>Higher service charges in beachfront towers</li><li>Less privacy compared to quieter waterfront communities</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose JBR?</h4><p>JBR is ideal for buyers and tenants seeking a high-energy beachfront lifestyle with constant demand and strong rental performance. It offers direct beach access, vibrant social life, and excellent connectivity to Marina and the wider city. For investors and lifestyle-driven end-users, JBR remains one of Dubai’s most liquid and recognisable waterfront districts.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'jumeirah-islands'), 'ae', 'Jumeirah Islands', 'An exclusive gated villa community offering spacious waterfront homes, lush landscaping, and a private island-style lifestyle minutes from Dubai Marina.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Jumeirah Islands is a prestigious, low-density residential community developed by Nakheel, located between Jumeirah Lake Towers and Sheikh Zayed Road. Comprising a series of man-made islands surrounded by lakes and greenery, the district is known for its privacy, spacious villas, and tranquil environment. It attracts families, senior professionals, and end-users seeking villa living in a central yet secluded setting.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>The community includes the Jumeirah Islands Pavilion, offering cafés, restaurants, supermarkets, and essential services. For a broader lifestyle experience, residents are minutes from Dubai Marina, JBR, and JLT, providing access to extensive dining, shopping, and nightlife options while keeping the home environment calm and residential.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>Outdoor living is a core feature of Jumeirah Islands. Landscaped walking paths, lakeside views, and quiet internal roads create a peaceful environment ideal for walking, jogging, and family activities. The community also offers a clubhouse, fitness facilities, and green open spaces, supporting a relaxed, resort-style lifestyle.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>Jumeirah Islands benefits from excellent road connectivity via Sheikh Zayed Road and Al Khail Road, allowing quick access to Dubai Marina, DIFC, Downtown Dubai, and major business hubs. The area is car-friendly, with metro stations available in nearby JLT and Dubai Marina.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>The district is popular with families due to proximity to international schools, nurseries, and educational institutions in JLT, Meadows, and Springs. Healthcare facilities, clinics, and hospitals are easily accessible in surrounding communities. Daily essentials are available within the Pavilion and nearby retail centres.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>Jumeirah Islands consists almost entirely of large detached villas, typically featuring private gardens, pools, and lake or skyline views. Architectural styles vary across clusters, offering a mix of contemporary and traditional designs. There are no apartments or townhouses within the community.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>The area enjoys strong long-term rental demand from families and executives seeking premium villa living close to business districts. Investment appeal is driven by limited supply, central location, and consistent end-user demand. Capital appreciation is supported by scarcity and the community’s established reputation.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>High entry prices compared to non-villa communities</li><li>Fully car-dependent lifestyle</li><li>Limited retail and entertainment within the community itself</li><li>Lower rental yields compared to high-density apartment districts</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Jumeirah Islands?</h4><p>Jumeirah Islands is ideal for buyers seeking privacy, space, and waterfront villa living in a central location. It offers a rare balance of exclusivity and accessibility, combining tranquil surroundings with proximity to Dubai’s major business and lifestyle hubs. For families and end-users prioritising long-term livability and capital preservation, it remains one of Dubai’s most desirable villa communities.</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'jumeirah-lakes-towers'), 'ae', 'Jumeirah Lakes Towers', 'A vibrant mixed-use district offering high-rise apartments, lake views, and strong rental demand with excellent metro connectivity next to Dubai Marina.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Jumeirah Lakes Towers (JLT) is a large-scale mixed-use district developed by DMCC, located opposite Dubai Marina along Sheikh Zayed Road. Built around a series of man-made lakes and landscaped parks, JLT combines residential towers, offices, retail, and dining within walkable clusters. The area attracts professionals, families, and investors seeking value-driven urban living with strong connectivity and consistent demand.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>JLT is known for its diverse dining scene, with cafés, casual restaurants, and independent eateries spread across the cluster podiums. Daily retail, supermarkets, gyms, and services are integrated at ground level. For nightlife and premium entertainment, residents are minutes from Dubai Marina and JBR, while JLT itself maintains a more community-led atmosphere.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>The lakeside promenades and central park areas provide walking and jogging paths, green spaces, and dog-friendly zones. Compared to many high-rise districts, JLT offers a more balanced outdoor experience, supporting an active and walkable lifestyle within the community.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>JLT is one of Dubai’s best-connected residential districts, served by multiple Dubai Metro stations along the Red Line, including DMCC and Sobha Realty stations. Direct access to Sheikh Zayed Road ensures fast travel across the city, while internal walkability reduces daily car reliance.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>The district is suitable for long-term living, with schools, nurseries, and healthcare facilities available in nearby communities such as JBR, Marina, and Al Sufouh. Clinics, pharmacies, supermarkets, gyms, and everyday services are widely available within JLT itself, supporting convenient day-to-day life.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>JLT is dominated by high-rise residential towers offering studios, one-bedroom, two-bedroom, and larger family apartments. Many units provide lake, skyline, or Marina views. Building quality and amenities vary by cluster, offering a wide range of price points within the same district.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>JLT is considered an investor favourite due to its relative affordability compared to Marina, strong metro access, and consistently high rental demand. Both long-term and short-term rentals perform well, particularly among professionals working in nearby business hubs. Capital appreciation is steady, supported by location and demand stability.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>Building quality and service charges vary by tower</li><li>Traffic congestion during peak hours on main access roads</li><li>Some clusters are busier than others</li><li>Less premium feel compared to Dubai Marina</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Jumeirah Lakes Towers?</h4><p>JLT is ideal for residents and investors seeking central living, strong transport links, and reliable rental performance without Marina-level pricing. It offers a balanced urban lifestyle with green spaces, walkability, and everyday convenience. For value-focused buyers prioritising yield, accessibility, and long-term demand, JLT remains one of Dubai’s most practical choices.</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'jumeirah-village-circle'), 'ae', 'Jumeirah Village Circle', 'A fast-growing master-planned community offering affordable apartments and townhouses, strong rental demand, and everyday convenience in a central suburban location.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Jumeirah Village Circle (JVC) is one of Dubai’s most popular residential communities, developed as a circular, master-planned neighbourhood between Al Khail Road and Sheikh Mohammed Bin Zayed Road. Known for its value-driven housing, wide streets, and expanding amenities, JVC attracts young professionals, families, and investors seeking affordability with central city access. The area continues to mature rapidly, with new buildings, retail, and infrastructure being added each year.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>JVC offers a growing number of cafés, casual restaurants, supermarkets, and local retail outlets spread throughout the community. Circle Mall serves as the main retail hub, providing shopping, dining, cinemas, and everyday services. Nightlife is limited, with residents typically visiting nearby areas such as Dubai Marina, JLT, or Downtown for entertainment.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>The community includes multiple parks, playgrounds, jogging paths, and landscaped green spaces distributed across its districts. JVC supports a more relaxed, suburban outdoor lifestyle, particularly suited to families, pet owners, and residents who value walkable neighbourhoods.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>JVC benefits from strong road connectivity via Al Khail Road, Sheikh Mohammed Bin Zayed Road, and Hessa Street, allowing efficient access to major business districts across Dubai. The area is primarily car-dependent, with public transport options continuing to improve as development progresses.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>JVC is increasingly family-friendly, with schools, nurseries, clinics, pharmacies, and healthcare centres either within or close to the community. Supermarkets, gyms, salons, and essential services are widely available, making it suitable for long-term residential living.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>The community offers a mix of mid-rise and high-rise apartment buildings, townhouses, and a limited number of villas. Properties range from studios to family-sized units, with newer developments featuring modern layouts and amenities. This variety allows buyers to enter at different price points.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>JVC is considered one of Dubai’s strongest areas for rental yield, driven by affordability, high tenant demand, and a constant influx of new residents. Both long-term and short-term rentals perform well, particularly in newer buildings. Capital appreciation potential is supported by ongoing development and infrastructure upgrades.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>Ongoing construction in certain areas</li><li>Traffic congestion during peak hours</li><li>Building quality varies between developers</li><li>Limited nightlife within the community</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Jumeirah Village Circle?</h4><p>JVC is ideal for buyers and investors seeking value, flexibility, and long-term growth. It offers accessible entry prices, strong rental performance, and improving lifestyle infrastructure in a central location. For first-time buyers, families, and yield-focused investors, JVC remains one of Dubai’s most versatile and in-demand communities.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'karama'), 'ae', 'Karama', 'A central, affordable residential district offering strong rental demand, excellent public transport access, and everyday convenience close to Dubai’s historic core.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Karama (Al Karama) is one of Dubai’s most established and densely populated residential neighbourhoods, located between Bur Dubai and Oud Metha. Known for its affordability, walkability, and central location, Karama has long been a hub for long-term residents, professionals, and families seeking value-driven urban living. The area has a strong community feel, supported by extensive retail, dining, and transport infrastructure.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>Karama is famous for its vibrant street-level retail, tailoring shops, cafés, and diverse dining scene offering cuisine from across the world. Karama Shopping Complex and surrounding streets cater to everyday needs, while larger malls such as Zabeel Park Mall and Dubai Mall are easily accessible. Nightlife is minimal and largely limited to nearby hotel lounges.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>While internal green spaces are limited, residents benefit from proximity to Zabeel Park, one of Dubai’s largest urban parks, offering jogging tracks, family areas, and open green lawns. Karama’s walkable layout also supports an active, car-light lifestyle.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>Karama is exceptionally well connected via multiple metro stations on the Red and Green Lines, including ADCB and BurJuman stations. Extensive bus routes and direct road access provide fast connectivity to Downtown Dubai, DIFC, and Deira. The area is one of the most public-transport-friendly districts in the city.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>Karama offers excellent access to schools, nurseries, clinics, hospitals, pharmacies, and government services in Bur Dubai and Oud Metha. Supermarkets, gyms, salons, and essential services are widely available within walking distance, making daily life highly convenient for residents.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>The district is dominated by low-rise and mid-rise apartment buildings offering studios, one-bedroom, and two-bedroom units. Properties focus on functionality and affordability rather than luxury finishes. New developments are limited, preserving the area’s established character.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>Karama is considered a high-demand rental area due to its central location and affordability. It attracts long-term tenants and professionals working across the city. Investors benefit from stable occupancy and reliable rental income, though capital appreciation is typically gradual.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>High-density living</li><li>Older building stock in many areas</li><li>Limited parking availability</li><li>Busy streets during peak hours</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Karama?</h4><p>Karama is ideal for residents and investors prioritising affordability, connectivity, and everyday practicality. It offers one of Dubai’s most walkable and transit-friendly environments, dependable rental demand, and a strong community atmosphere. For value-focused buyers seeking central exposure without premium pricing, Karama remains a proven and resilient choice.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'mirdif'), 'ae', 'Mirdif', 'A well-established suburban district offering spacious villas, family-friendly living, and everyday convenience near Dubai International Airport.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Mirdif is one of Dubai’s most established suburban residential districts, located in the eastern part of the city near Mushrif Park and Dubai International Airport. Known for its low-rise character and strong community feel, Mirdif has long been popular with families and long-term residents seeking space, affordability, and a quieter lifestyle away from high-density urban centres. The area blends traditional neighbourhoods with newer residential clusters and retail hubs.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>Mirdif City Centre is the main lifestyle anchor, offering a wide range of retail outlets, supermarkets, cinemas, cafés, and restaurants. Additional local dining and retail options are spread throughout the neighbourhood. Nightlife is minimal, with the area focused on family-oriented, everyday living rather than entertainment-driven activity.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>Mirdif benefits from proximity to Mushrif Park, one of Dubai’s largest natural parks, offering cycling tracks, walking trails, picnic areas, and open green space. Within residential areas, quiet streets and neighbourhood parks support outdoor activity and a relaxed suburban lifestyle.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>The district is well connected via Sheikh Mohammed Bin Zayed Road and Al Khawaneej Road, providing access to Deira, Downtown Dubai, and Sharjah. While there is no direct metro access within Mirdif, road connectivity is strong, and the area is highly car-friendly.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>Mirdif is particularly popular with families due to the availability of schools, nurseries, and educational institutions offering international curricula. Healthcare access is strong, with clinics, hospitals, and pharmacies located within or near the community. Supermarkets, gyms, salons, and essential services are widely available, supporting comfortable long-term living.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>The district is dominated by villas, townhouses, and low-rise apartment buildings. Properties typically offer larger layouts and private outdoor space compared to many newer developments. Architectural styles range from traditional to more modern designs in newer clusters.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>Mirdif attracts steady long-term rental demand from families and professionals seeking suburban living at accessible price points. Investment appeal is primarily income-focused, with stable occupancy and gradual capital appreciation supported by consistent end-user demand.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>Car dependency for most daily activities</li><li>Limited nightlife and lifestyle attractions</li><li>Aircraft noise in some areas due to airport proximity</li><li>Fewer new or off-plan developments</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Mirdif?</h4><p>Mirdif is ideal for families and residents seeking space, affordability, and a strong neighbourhood feel. It offers practical suburban living with good schools, green spaces, and everyday amenities, all within easy reach of central Dubai. For end-users prioritising livability and long-term stability, Mirdif remains a dependable and well-loved community.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'naif'), 'ae', 'Naif', 'A central Deira district offering highly affordable apartment living, strong rental demand, and excellent access to traditional markets and public transport.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Naif is one of Dubai’s oldest and most densely populated districts, located in the heart of Deira near Dubai Creek. Historically a trading and commercial hub, the area today blends residential buildings with wholesale markets, retail streets, and traditional souks. Naif attracts residents and investors focused on affordability, centrality, and access to Deira’s commercial activity rather than lifestyle-led living.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>Naif is well known for Naif Souk and surrounding retail streets offering textiles, perfumes, electronics, and everyday goods at competitive prices. Dining options are plentiful and affordable, with a wide range of local cafés and international eateries catering to daily needs. Nightlife is minimal and mostly limited to nearby hotel lounges in Deira.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>The district itself has limited green spaces, reflecting its dense urban nature. However, residents benefit from proximity to Dubai Creek promenades, abra crossings, and nearby public parks in Deira and Bur Dubai. The lifestyle is functional and walkable rather than recreational.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>Naif is extremely well connected via public transport. Baniyas Square Metro Station (Green Line) is within walking distance, supported by extensive bus routes and abra services across Dubai Creek. Major roads provide access to Deira, Bur Dubai, and Sharjah, making it a highly practical base for commuters.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>Everyday living in Naif is supported by nearby schools, clinics, pharmacies, and hospitals across Deira. Supermarkets, convenience stores, gyms, and essential services are widely available within walking distance, making the area suitable for budget-conscious long-term residents.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>Naif is dominated by low-rise and mid-rise apartment buildings, many of which are older and designed for affordability. Units typically include studios and one-bedroom apartments, with functional layouts rather than modern amenities.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>The area is popular with income-focused investors due to very low entry prices and consistently high rental demand. Occupancy levels are typically strong, driven by affordability and proximity to employment zones. Capital appreciation is modest, with the main appeal being rental yield rather than long-term price growth.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>High-density living</li><li>Older building quality</li><li>Limited green spaces</li><li>Busy commercial environment</li><li>Less suitable for families seeking suburban amenities</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Naif?</h4><p>Naif is ideal for residents and investors prioritising affordability, central access, and strong rental demand. It offers one of the lowest entry points in Dubai’s property market while benefiting from excellent transport connectivity and constant tenant demand. For yield-focused investors and value-driven residents, Naif remains a practical and resilient option.</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'oud-metha'), 'ae', 'Oud Metha', 'A central, well-established district offering spacious apartments and villas, strong healthcare and education infrastructure, and excellent connectivity in the heart of Dubai.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Oud Metha, also officially known as Umm Hurair, is a centrally located residential district in Bur Dubai, positioned between Karama, Al Garhoud, and Downtown Dubai. The area is known for its calm residential streets, medical facilities, and educational institutions, making it popular with families, professionals, and long-term residents. It offers a balanced urban lifestyle with more space and greenery than older high-density districts.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>The district offers a range of local cafés, casual restaurants, supermarkets, and everyday retail outlets serving residents’ daily needs. Larger lifestyle destinations such as Wafi Mall, Dubai Festival City Mall, and Downtown Dubai are a short drive away. Nightlife is minimal and largely limited to nearby hotel lounges, keeping the area residential and quiet.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>Oud Metha benefits from proximity to Zabeel Park and Dubai Creek waterfront areas, providing access to jogging tracks, green spaces, and family-friendly outdoor activities. The neighbourhood itself features wider streets and lower density, supporting walking and everyday outdoor movement.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>The area is very well connected via Oud Metha and Health Care City Metro Stations on the Green Line, offering direct access to Deira, Bur Dubai, and central Dubai. Major roads such as Sheikh Rashid Road and Oud Metha Road provide efficient connectivity across the city. The district is both car-friendly and public-transport accessible.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>Oud Metha is widely recognised as one of Dubai’s key healthcare and education hubs. It is home to several major hospitals, clinics, and medical centres, as well as reputable schools, colleges, and universities. Supermarkets, gyms, pharmacies, and essential services are plentiful, supporting comfortable long-term living for families and professionals.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>The district offers a mix of low-rise apartment buildings, mid-rise residential blocks, and a limited number of villas. Properties are known for larger layouts compared to newer developments, with a focus on functionality, privacy, and long-term livability.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>Oud Metha enjoys steady long-term rental demand driven by its central location, proximity to hospitals and schools, and family-friendly environment. Investment appeal is income-focused, with stable occupancy and gradual capital appreciation rather than rapid price growth.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>Older building stock in some parts of the district</li><li>Limited nightlife and entertainment within the area</li><li>Parking constraints in certain streets</li><li>Fewer new or off-plan developments</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Oud Metha / Umm Hurair?</h4><p>Oud Metha is ideal for residents seeking centrality, space, and a calm residential environment. It offers excellent access to healthcare, education, and transport while maintaining a quieter feel than surrounding districts. For families, professionals, and long-term investors prioritising livability and stability, it remains one of Dubai’s most dependable central neighbourhoods.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'palm-jumeirah'), 'ae', 'Palm Jumeirah', 'Dubai’s most iconic waterfront destination offering ultra-luxury villas and apartments, private beach access, and world-class resort-style living.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Palm Jumeirah is one of the world’s most recognisable man-made islands and Dubai’s flagship luxury residential destination. Shaped like a palm tree extending into the Arabian Gulf, the community features a mix of private beachfront villas, high-end apartment buildings, and five-star hotels. Palm Jumeirah attracts high-net-worth individuals, international buyers, and investors seeking exclusivity, prestige, and long-term capital value in a globally recognised location.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>Palm Jumeirah offers an exceptional lifestyle experience, with fine dining restaurants, beach clubs, cafés, and luxury hotels spread across the trunk and crescent. Nakheel Mall serves as the island’s main retail hub, providing shopping, dining, entertainment, and everyday services. Nightlife is premium and lifestyle-led, centred around hotel lounges and beachfront venues rather than high-density club scenes.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>Beach access is central to life on Palm Jumeirah. Residents enjoy private beaches, swimming, water sports, jogging tracks, and waterfront promenades. The island’s layout supports a resort-style outdoor lifestyle, with uninterrupted sea views and direct access to the Gulf from many homes.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>Palm Jumeirah is accessible via Palm Jumeirah Road and Sheikh Zayed Road, with the Palm Monorail connecting the trunk to Atlantis and linking to Dubai Tram for wider city access. While the island is primarily car-dependent, connectivity to Dubai Marina, Media City, and DIFC is efficient.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>While Palm Jumeirah is not a traditional family suburb, residents have access to international schools, nurseries, and healthcare facilities in nearby areas such as Dubai Marina, Al Sufouh, and Jumeirah. Clinics, gyms, supermarkets, and concierge-level services are widely available within residential buildings and hotels.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>Palm Jumeirah offers a diverse mix of property types, including signature beachfront villas, garden homes, townhouses, luxury apartments, and branded residences. Many properties feature private pools, direct beach access, and panoramic sea or skyline views. Architectural styles range from contemporary to Mediterranean-inspired designs.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>Palm Jumeirah is one of Dubai’s strongest markets for both capital appreciation and high-end rental demand. It performs exceptionally well for short-term and holiday rentals, driven by global tourism and limited beachfront supply. Long-term rental demand remains strong among executives and international residents seeking premium waterfront living.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>Very high entry prices</li><li>Premium service charges in luxury developments</li><li>Car dependency for daily commuting</li><li>Tourist activity in hotel-heavy areas</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Palm Jumeirah?</h4><p>Palm Jumeirah is ideal for buyers seeking global prestige, beachfront living, and long-term value preservation. It offers unmatched lifestyle appeal, strong international demand, and limited comparable supply. For investors and end-users prioritising exclusivity, waterfront access, and brand recognition, Palm Jumeirah remains one of the most desirable addresses in the world.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'port-saeed'), 'ae', 'Port Saeed', 'A centrally located Deira district offering practical apartment living, strong transport connectivity, and immediate access to Dubai Creek, malls, and business hubs.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Port Saeed is a well-established mixed-use district in Deira, located along Dubai Creek and adjacent to key commercial and transport zones. Known for its strategic location near Deira City Centre and Dubai International Airport, the area blends residential buildings, offices, and retail. Port Saeed attracts professionals, long-term residents, and investors seeking centrality, convenience, and consistent rental demand.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>Deira City Centre Mall is the main lifestyle anchor for Port Saeed, offering extensive retail, supermarkets, dining, cinemas, and entertainment. The district itself includes cafés, casual restaurants, and local retail outlets catering to daily needs. Nightlife is limited and primarily hotel-based, with residents relying on nearby areas for evening entertainment.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>Residents benefit from proximity to Dubai Creek, offering waterfront promenades, walking paths, and scenic views. While internal green spaces are limited, nearby parks and Creek-side areas provide accessible outdoor options. The lifestyle is urban and practical rather than recreation-led.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>Port Saeed is one of Deira’s best-connected districts, served by Deira City Centre and Al Rigga Metro Stations on the Red Line, along with extensive bus routes. The area offers quick access to Dubai International Airport and major roads connecting to Downtown Dubai, DIFC, and Bur Dubai.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>The district offers easy access to schools, nurseries, clinics, hospitals, and government services across Deira and nearby Garhoud. Supermarkets, gyms, pharmacies, and everyday services are widely available, supporting comfortable long-term living.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>Port Saeed is dominated by low-rise and mid-rise apartment buildings offering studios, one-bedroom, and two-bedroom units. Properties are generally functional and value-oriented, with some newer buildings offering modern layouts near the Creek and major roads.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>The area benefits from strong long-term rental demand due to its central location, metro access, and proximity to commercial zones and the airport. Investment appeal is income-focused, with stable occupancy and gradual capital appreciation rather than rapid price growth.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>Older building stock in some areas</li><li>Limited green spaces within the district</li><li>Busy streets during peak hours</li><li>Less lifestyle-driven compared to newer waterfront developments</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Port Saeed?</h4><p>Port Saeed is ideal for residents and investors prioritising connectivity, convenience, and central living. It offers dependable rental demand, excellent transport links, and immediate access to major retail and employment hubs. For value-focused buyers seeking central exposure in Deira, Port Saeed remains a practical and reliable choice.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'riggat-al-buteen'), 'ae', 'Riggat Al Buteen', 'A quiet, upscale waterfront neighbourhood in Deira offering spacious villas and apartments, creekside living, and excellent connectivity near the city centre.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Riggat Al Buteen is a low-density, waterfront residential district in Deira, located along Dubai Creek near Port Saeed and Al Garhoud. Known for its calm streets, greenery, and premium residential feel, the area stands out from the busier parts of Deira. It attracts families, senior professionals, and long-term residents seeking space, privacy, and central access without high-rise congestion.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>Riggat Al Buteen is primarily residential, with limited retail and dining within the neighbourhood itself. Residents rely on nearby lifestyle hubs such as Deira City Centre, Dubai Festival City Mall, and waterfront hotel restaurants for shopping and dining. Nightlife is minimal and discreet, reinforcing the area’s quiet residential character.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>Creekside living is a key feature of Riggat Al Buteen. Residents enjoy waterfront views, walking paths, and a peaceful environment ideal for jogging and daily outdoor activity. Tree-lined streets and low traffic contribute to a relaxed, community-oriented outdoor lifestyle.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>The district offers excellent road connectivity via Al Ittihad Road and Sheikh Rashid Road, providing quick access to Downtown Dubai, DIFC, and Dubai International Airport. Nearby metro stations in Deira and Garhoud support public transport access, while the area remains highly car-friendly.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>Riggat Al Buteen benefits from proximity to reputable schools, nurseries, and healthcare facilities in Deira, Garhoud, and Festival City. Clinics, hospitals, supermarkets, and daily services are easily accessible, making the area well suited for long-term family living.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>The district features a mix of large villas, townhouses, and low-rise apartment buildings. Properties are known for generous layouts, private gardens in villa plots, and a more premium residential feel compared to other Deira neighbourhoods. High-rise towers are notably absent.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>Riggat Al Buteen attracts steady long-term rental demand from families and executives seeking central yet quiet living. Investment appeal is primarily end-user and income-focused, with stable yields and gradual capital appreciation supported by limited supply and waterfront positioning.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>Limited retail and dining within the district</li><li>Older villa stock in some pockets</li><li>Car dependency for daily errands</li><li>Less suitable for buyers seeking high-density apartment living</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Riggat Al Buteen?</h4><p>Riggat Al Buteen is ideal for buyers seeking calm, space, and waterfront proximity in a central Dubai location. It offers a rare blend of Deira accessibility with a premium residential atmosphere, making it especially appealing to families and long-term end-users. For those prioritising privacy, greenery, and long-term livability, it remains one of Deira’s most refined residential enclaves.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'trade-centre'), 'ae', 'Trade Centre', 'A prime central business district offering high-rise living, exceptional connectivity, and immediate access to DIFC, Downtown Dubai, and Sheikh Zayed Road.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Trade Centre is a central Dubai district built around the Dubai World Trade Centre and Sheikh Zayed Road corridor. It serves as a strategic link between DIFC, Downtown Dubai, and older parts of the city such as Bur Dubai. The area combines commercial towers, residential high-rises, hotels, and exhibition venues, attracting professionals, executives, and investors seeking city-centre convenience and strong rental demand.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>Trade Centre benefits from proximity to DIFC, Downtown Dubai, and City Walk, offering access to some of Dubai’s best dining, retail, and entertainment destinations. Within the district, residents have access to cafés, hotel restaurants, and everyday retail. Nightlife is primarily centred in nearby DIFC and Downtown rather than within the district itself.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>Zabeel Park lies adjacent to Trade Centre, providing one of the area’s biggest lifestyle advantages. Residents enjoy access to jogging tracks, green spaces, and open lawns, offering a rare balance of greenery in a high-density central district.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>Trade Centre is one of Dubai’s most connected areas. World Trade Centre and Emirates Towers Metro Stations on the Red Line provide direct access to Dubai Marina, Downtown Dubai, and Dubai International Airport. Sheikh Zayed Road offers fast road connectivity, making the district ideal for both car owners and public transport users.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>While not primarily family-suburban, Trade Centre residents benefit from nearby schools, nurseries, and healthcare facilities in DIFC, Downtown, and Al Wasl. Clinics, gyms, supermarkets, and daily services are widely available within residential towers and surrounding mixed-use developments.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>The district is dominated by high-rise apartment towers and serviced residences offering studios, one-bedroom, and two-bedroom units, along with select larger apartments. Many properties offer skyline or Sheikh Zayed Road views and include shared amenities such as pools, gyms, and concierge services.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>Trade Centre is considered a strong rental market due to its central location, metro access, and proximity to major business hubs. Long-term rental demand is driven by professionals working in DIFC, Downtown, and along Sheikh Zayed Road. Investment appeal is income-focused, with stable yields and solid liquidity.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>Busy environment during exhibitions and events</li><li>Traffic congestion during peak hours</li><li>High service charges in some towers</li><li>Limited low-density or family-style housing</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Trade Centre?</h4><p>Trade Centre is ideal for professionals and investors seeking centrality, connectivity, and consistent rental demand. It offers unmatched access to Dubai’s core business districts, metro connectivity, and major landmarks. For buyers prioritising convenience, yield stability, and city-centre exposure, Trade Centre remains a highly strategic choice.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'umm-suqeim'), 'ae', 'Umm Suqeim', 'A prestigious low-rise coastal district offering spacious villas, beach access, and a refined family-oriented lifestyle close to Dubai’s key leisure and business hubs.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Umm Suqeim is one of Dubai’s most established and desirable coastal residential districts, stretching along the coastline near Jumeirah, Al Wasl, and Al Sufouh. Known for its quiet streets, villa-focused living, and proximity to the beach, the area attracts families, professionals, and long-term residents seeking space, privacy, and a premium lifestyle in a central location.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>Umm Suqeim offers a curated selection of cafés, boutique restaurants, and casual dining spots along Jumeirah Road and surrounding streets. Popular lifestyle destinations such as Madinat Jumeirah, Mall of the Emirates, City Walk, and Kite Beach are nearby, offering expanded shopping and entertainment. Nightlife is understated and centred around hotel lounges and beachside venues rather than clubs.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>Beach access is a defining feature of Umm Suqeim, with multiple public beaches, jogging tracks, and cycling paths along the coast. Residents enjoy an active outdoor lifestyle supported by nearby parks, open spaces, and coastal walkways. Kite Beach and Jumeirah Beach Park add to the area’s recreational appeal.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>The district is well connected via Jumeirah Road, Al Wasl Road, and Sheikh Zayed Road, offering efficient access to Downtown Dubai, DIFC, and Dubai Marina. Umm Suqeim is primarily car-friendly, with public transport available via bus routes and nearby metro stations in adjacent districts.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>Umm Suqeim is highly family-friendly, with access to top international schools, nurseries, and educational institutions. Healthcare facilities, clinics, and hospitals are widely available nearby, including premium private medical centres. Supermarkets, gyms, salons, and essential services are conveniently located throughout the area.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>The area is dominated by large villas, townhouses, and a limited number of low-rise apartment buildings. Properties typically feature private gardens, generous plot sizes, and quiet residential streets. New supply is limited, preserving the area’s low-density, premium character.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>Umm Suqeim appeals strongly to end-users and long-term tenants seeking coastal villa living. Rental demand is stable, particularly among families and senior professionals. Investment appeal is focused on long-term capital preservation and scarcity rather than short-term rental yields.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>High entry prices compared to non-coastal areas</li><li>Limited availability of new or off-plan developments</li><li>Car dependency for daily commuting</li><li>Villa-focused market may not suit apartment-only buyers</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Umm Suqeim?</h4><p>Umm Suqeim is ideal for buyers seeking a premium coastal lifestyle with space, privacy, and long-term stability. It offers beach access, family-oriented living, and proximity to Dubai’s top leisure destinations without the density of high-rise districts. For end-users prioritising quality of life and capital preservation, Umm Suqeim remains one of Dubai’s most enduring residential choices.</p><p>‍</p>'),
((SELECT id FROM public.communities WHERE slug = 'zabeel'), 'ae', 'Zabeel', 'A prestigious central district offering low-density residential living, expansive green spaces, and immediate access to DIFC, Downtown Dubai, and Sheikh Zayed Road.', '<h4>1. Community overview</h4><p>Zabeel is one of Dubai’s most strategically located and refined central districts, positioned between DIFC, Downtown Dubai, and Bur Dubai. Known for its wide roads, greenery, and proximity to government and landmark areas, Zabeel blends premium residential neighbourhoods with major city infrastructure. The district appeals to families, professionals, and end-users seeking space, privacy, and centrality without the intensity of high-rise living.</p><h4>2. Lifestyle &amp; amenities</h4><p><strong>Shopping, dining &amp; nightlife</strong><br>Zabeel residents benefit from immediate access to DIFC, Downtown Dubai, and City Walk, offering some of the city’s best dining, retail, and entertainment options. Within the district itself, amenities are limited and discreet, reinforcing Zabeel’s calm, residential character. Larger retail destinations such as Dubai Mall and Wafi Mall are only minutes away.</p><p><strong>Outdoor &amp; recreation</strong><br>Zabeel Park is one of the district’s defining features and one of Dubai’s largest urban parks. It offers jogging tracks, cycling paths, open lawns, children’s play areas, and event spaces. The park provides a rare green buffer in the city centre, supporting an active, family-friendly outdoor lifestyle.</p><p><strong>Transport &amp; connectivity</strong><br>Zabeel is exceptionally well connected via Sheikh Zayed Road, Sheikh Rashid Road, and Al Khail Road. World Trade Centre and Max Metro Stations on the Red Line provide public transport access, while major road links allow fast travel to DIFC, Downtown Dubai, Dubai International Airport, and beyond. The area is highly car-friendly while remaining centrally accessible.</p><h4>3. Schools, healthcare &amp; everyday living</h4><p>The district benefits from proximity to reputable schools, nurseries, and universities in Oud Metha, Jumeirah, and Downtown Dubai. Healthcare access is strong, with hospitals and clinics located nearby. Supermarkets, gyms, pharmacies, and essential services are easily accessible within surrounding mixed-use developments.</p><h4>4. Property market &amp; housing types</h4><p><strong>Housing typologies</strong><br>Zabeel offers a mix of premium villas, low-rise residential buildings, and select high-end apartment developments, particularly in newer sub-areas such as Zabeel 1. Properties are characterised by larger layouts, privacy, and lower density compared to neighbouring districts.</p><p><strong>Investment / rental appeal</strong><br>Zabeel appeals primarily to end-users and long-term tenants seeking central living with space and greenery. Rental demand is stable, driven by proximity to DIFC and Downtown, while investment appeal is focused on long-term capital preservation supported by location scarcity and limited residential supply.</p><h4>5. Things to consider / potential drawbacks</h4><ul><li>Limited retail and dining within the district itself</li><li>Premium pricing due to central location</li><li>Car dependency for most daily errands</li><li>Limited new or off-plan residential supply</li></ul><h4>6. Why choose Zabeel?</h4><p>Zabeel is ideal for buyers seeking a calm, prestigious residential environment in the heart of Dubai. It offers immediate access to business hubs, expansive green space through Zabeel Park, and a low-density lifestyle rarely found in such a central location. For families and professionals prioritising space, privacy, and long-term value, Zabeel remains one of Dubai’s most understated prime districts.</p><p>‍</p>')
